window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-plane","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Motion in a Plane · Practice","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector of magnitude 8 units makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the x-axis and $45^\\circ$ with the y-axis. It makes an acute angle with the z-axis. What is its z-component?","image":"","options":["2.83 units","5.66 units","4 units","6.93 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: A = 8, $\\alpha = 60^\\circ$, $\\beta = 45^\\circ$.\nFrom $A^2 = A_x^2 + A_y^2 + A_z^2$ with $A_x = A\\cos\\alpha$ and so on, we get $\\cos^2\\alpha + \\cos^2\\beta + \\cos^2\\gamma = 1$.\nSubstituting: $\\cos^2\\gamma = 1 - \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$. Since $\\gamma$ is acute, $\\cos\\gamma = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nSo $A_z = A\\cos\\gamma = 8 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 4$ units.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"In 'uniform circular motion', the word 'uniform' refers to which quantity being constant?","image":"","options":["Velocity","Speed","Acceleration vector","Position vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines uniform circular motion as motion along a circular path at constant speed. The word 'uniform' refers to the speed.\nThe velocity is not constant, because its direction changes continuously along the tangent.\nThe acceleration vector keeps turning towards the centre, and the position vector also keeps rotating.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A constant force of 6 N acting due east is applied on a body for 3 s. Which option correctly describes the product of the time interval (3 s) and the force?","image":"","options":["18 N s directed due east; it is a vector","18 N s; it is a scalar because time is a scalar","2 N s$^{-1}$ directed due east; it is a vector","18 N s directed due west; multiplication by time reverses direction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Multiplying a vector by a positive scalar λ multiplies its magnitude by λ and keeps its direction.\nIf λ has its own dimension, the dimension of the product is the product of the two dimensions.\nSo 3 s × 6 N (east) = 18 N s due east. This is impulse, which is a vector.\nOption (2) is wrong: the product of a scalar and a vector is a vector, not a scalar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle has initial velocity $\\vec{v}_0 = (2\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m/s and a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = -1.0\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$. What is its speed at the instant its velocity is perpendicular to its initial velocity?","image":"","options":["2.24 m/s","4.47 m/s","1.0 m/s","3.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t = 2\\hat{i} + (4 - t)\\hat{j}$.\nCondition for perpendicular velocities: $\\vec{v} \\cdot \\vec{v}_0 = 0$.\nSubstituting: $2(2) + 4(4 - t) = 0$, so $20 - 4t = 0$ and t = 5 s.\nThen $\\vec{v} = 2\\hat{i} - 1\\hat{j}$ m/s.\nSo speed $= \\sqrt{4 + 1} = \\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.24$ m/s.\nThe option 4.47 m/s is the initial speed $\\sqrt{20}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"For a projectile launched obliquely from level ground (air resistance neglected), how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The horizontal component of its velocity remains constant.\n(ii) The time taken to rise to the maximum height equals the time taken to fall back to the launch level.\n(iii) Its path is a parabola.\n(iv) At the highest point, its velocity and acceleration are parallel to each other.\n(v) If the initial speed is doubled at the same angle, the maximum height also doubles.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $a_x = 0$, so $v_x$ does not change.\n(ii) Correct: $T_f = 2t_m$, by the symmetry of the path.\n(iii) Correct: $y = (\\tan\\theta_0)x - \\frac{g x^2}{2(v_0\\cos\\theta_0)^2}$ has the form $y = ax + bx^2$, which is a parabola.\n(iv) Incorrect: at the top, $\\vec{v}$ is horizontal and $\\vec{g}$ is vertical, so they are perpendicular.\n(v) Incorrect: $h_m \\propto v_0^2$, so it becomes four times.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two balls are projected from level ground with the same speed, one at $30^\\circ$ and the other at $60^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Neglect air resistance.\nStatement I: The two balls have equal horizontal ranges.\nStatement II: The two balls reach equal maximum heights.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $R \\propto \\sin 2\\theta_0$, and $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\sin 120^\\circ$. The angles $45^\\circ \\pm 15^\\circ$ give equal ranges.\nStatement II is incorrect. $h_m \\propto \\sin^2\\theta_0$, so the ratio of heights is $\\sin^2 30^\\circ : \\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} : \\frac{3}{4} = 1 : 3$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector $\\vec{A}$ is multiplied by the real number $-1.5$. Which of the following correctly describes the resulting vector?","image":"","options":["Magnitude $1.5|\\vec{A}|$, direction same as $\\vec{A}$","Magnitude $1.5|\\vec{A}|$, direction opposite to $\\vec{A}$","Magnitude $-1.5|\\vec{A}|$, direction same as $\\vec{A}$","Magnitude $|\\vec{A}|/1.5$, direction opposite to $\\vec{A}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Multiplying a vector by a negative number $-\\lambda$ reverses its direction and scales its magnitude by $\\lambda$.\nHere $\\lambda = 1.5$, so the new magnitude is $1.5|\\vec{A}|$ and the direction is opposite to $\\vec{A}$.\nA magnitude can never be negative, so option (3) is wrong. Option (1) ignores the reversal of direction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector in three-dimensional space makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the x-axis and $45^\\circ$ with the y-axis. What acute angle does it make with the z-axis?","image":"","options":["$45^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\alpha = 60^\\circ$, $\\beta = 45^\\circ$.\nFormula: $A_x = A\\cos\\alpha$, $A_y = A\\cos\\beta$, $A_z = A\\cos\\gamma$. Using $A^2 = A_x^2+A_y^2+A_z^2$ gives $\\cos^2\\alpha + \\cos^2\\beta + \\cos^2\\gamma = 1$.\nSubstituting: $\\cos^2\\gamma = 1 - \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$, so $\\cos\\gamma = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nSo the acute angle is $\\gamma = 60^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Over any time interval, the average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of its average velocity.\nReason (R): The path length between two points is always greater than or equal to the magnitude of the displacement between them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Average speed = path length / time and |average velocity| = |displacement| / time, both over the same interval.\nR is true. Displacement is the straight-line separation of the end points, and no path can be shorter than it.\nDividing both quantities in R by the same time interval gives A directly. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moving in the x-y plane is at the point (2 m, 3 m) at t = 1 s and at the point (8 m, 11 m) at t = 3 s. What is the magnitude of its displacement over this interval?","image":"","options":["10 m","14 m","13.6 m","5 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: initial position (2, 3) m and final position (8, 11) m.\nFormula: $\\Delta \\vec{r} = \\Delta x\\,\\hat{i} + \\Delta y\\,\\hat{j}$ and $|\\Delta \\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{(\\Delta x)^2 + (\\Delta y)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta x = 8 - 2 = 6$ m and $\\Delta y = 11 - 3 = 8$ m, so $|\\Delta \\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{36 + 64}$.\nSo the magnitude of the displacement is 10 m. Adding 6 and 8 gives the wrong value of 14 m, and 13.6 m is only the magnitude of the final position vector.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"At a junction, a wire carrying 3 A meets a wire carrying 4 A, the two wires being at right angles to each other. The current in the outgoing wire is 7 A, not 5 A. Which conclusion about electric current does this support?","image":"","options":["Current is a vector, and the junction violates the parallelogram law","Current is a scalar because, although it has a direction, it does not combine by the triangle or parallelogram law","Current is a vector whose magnitude here is 5 A, and 7 A results from measurement error","Current is a scalar because it has no direction associated with it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A vector needs both magnitude and direction, and it must obey the triangle or parallelogram law of addition.\nIf current were a vector, 3 A and 4 A at right angles would combine to $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = 5$ A.\nThe observed value is 3 + 4 = 7 A, which is ordinary algebraic addition. So current is a scalar.\nOption (4) fails because current does have a sense of flow along the wire.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A non-zero vector $\\vec{A}$ is multiplied by different real numbers $\\lambda$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (value of $\\lambda$):\n(P) $\\lambda = 2$\n(Q) $\\lambda = -1$\n(R) $\\lambda = 0$\n(S) $\\lambda = -0.5$\nList-II (resulting vector $\\lambda\\vec{A}$):\n(1) Null vector\n(2) Magnitude $|\\vec{A}|/2$, opposite to $\\vec{A}$\n(3) Magnitude $2|\\vec{A}|$, along $\\vec{A}$\n(4) Magnitude $|\\vec{A}|$, opposite to $\\vec{A}$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) $\\lambda = 2 > 0$: magnitude is doubled and the direction is unchanged, so P-3.\n(Q) $\\lambda = -1$: same magnitude, reversed direction (this is $-\\vec{A}$), so Q-4.\n(R) $\\lambda = 0$: $0\\vec{A} = \\vec{0}$, the null vector, so R-1.\n(S) $\\lambda = -0.5$: half the magnitude and reversed direction, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are vector quantities?\nDisplacement, mass, velocity, temperature, acceleration, force, time, distance","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A vector has both magnitude and direction and obeys the triangle (parallelogram) law of addition.\nVectors: displacement, velocity, acceleration and force, which makes 4.\nScalars: mass, temperature, time and distance, which have magnitude only.\nDistance is the most tempting wrong pick. It is a path length and carries no direction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle has initial velocity $\\vec{v}_0 = (3.0\\,\\hat{i} + 4.0\\,\\hat{j})$ m/s. It moves with a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = -2.0\\,\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$. What is the speed of the particle at the instant its velocity first becomes perpendicular to its initial velocity?","image":"","options":["3.75 m/s","3.0 m/s","5.0 m/s","2.25 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t = 3\\hat{i} + (4 - 2t)\\hat{j}$.\nCondition: $\\vec{v}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{v}_0$ when $\\vec{v}\\cdot\\vec{v}_0 = 0$, i.e. $3(3) + 4(4 - 2t) = 0$. This gives $t = 25/8 = 3.125$ s.\nSubstituting: $v_x = 3$ m/s and $v_y = 4 - 6.25 = -2.25$ m/s.\nSo speed $= \\sqrt{3^2 + 2.25^2} = 3.75$ m/s. The option 3.0 m/s is the speed at the top of the path ($v_y = 0$), not at the perpendicular instant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Particle A moves uniformly on a circle of radius R with time period T. Particle B moves uniformly on a circle of radius 4R with time period 2T.\nStatement I: The magnitudes of the centripetal accelerations of A and B are equal.\nStatement II: The linear speeds of A and B are in the ratio 1 : 2.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Centripetal acceleration: $a_c = \\omega^2 R = \\frac{4\\pi^2 R}{T^2}$.\nFor A: $\\frac{4\\pi^2 R}{T^2}$. For B: $\\frac{4\\pi^2 (4R)}{(2T)^2} = \\frac{4\\pi^2 R}{T^2}$. The two are equal, so Statement I is correct.\nLinear speed: $v = \\frac{2\\pi R}{T}$.\nFor A: $\\frac{2\\pi R}{T}$. For B: $\\frac{2\\pi (4R)}{2T} = \\frac{4\\pi R}{T}$. The ratio is 1 : 2, so Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle moving along a straight line can have an average speed greater than the magnitude of its average velocity over some time interval.\nReason (R): For motion along a straight line, the path length is always equal to the magnitude of the displacement.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Suppose a particle goes 10 m forward and then 4 m back along the same line. The path length is 14 m but |displacement| is only 6 m, so average speed exceeds |average velocity|.\nR is false. Path length equals |displacement| only if the object does not change its direction of motion. Being on a straight line is not enough, because the object can reverse.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin at t = 0 with velocity $6\\hat{i}$ m/s. It moves under a constant acceleration $(-2\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. What is its y-coordinate at t = 3 s?","image":"","options":["18 m","9 m","36 m","12 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $y_0 = 0$, $v_{0y} = 0$, $a_y = 4$ m/s$^2$, t = 3 s.\nFormula: $y = y_0 + v_{0y}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_y t^2$.\nSubstituting: $y = 0 + 0 + \\frac{1}{2}(4)(3)^2 = 18$ m.\nThe x-motion (initial velocity 6 m/s, acceleration $-2$ m/s$^2$) does not affect y.\nSo y = 18 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two forces of magnitudes 3 N and 5 N act on a particle. The angle between them is $60^\\circ$. What is the magnitude of their resultant?","image":"","options":["7 N","8 N","5.83 N","4.36 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 3$ N, $B = 5$ N, $\\theta = 60^\\circ$.\nFormula (law of cosines): $R^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $R^2 = 9 + 25 + 2(3)(5)(0.5) = 49$.\nSo $R = 7$ N.\nThe value 4.36 N is what you get with $\\theta = 120^\\circ$ (or by subtracting the cosine term), and 8 N is the plain sum, valid only for $\\theta = 0^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is launched from level ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta_0$ above the horizontal (air resistance negligible). Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Time of flight\n(B) Maximum height\n(C) Horizontal range\n(D) Maximum possible range for the given $v_0$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g}$\n(ii) $\\frac{v_0^2}{g}$\n(iii) $\\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(iv) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Set $y=0$ to get $T_f = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$, which is (iii).\n(B) Substitute $t_m = \\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$ to get $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$, which is (i).\n(C) $R = (v_0\\cos\\theta_0)T_f = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin2\\theta_0}{g}$, which is (iv).\n(D) The range is maximum at $\\theta_0 = 45^\\circ$, giving $\\frac{v_0^2}{g}$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two balls are thrown from the same point on level ground with equal speeds but at different angles of projection. Both land at the same point, $40$ m away. Neglecting air resistance, what is the product of their times of flight? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["8 s$^2$","4 s$^2$","16 s$^2$","2 s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Equal speed and equal range with different angles means the angles are $\\theta$ and $90^\\circ - \\theta$.\nTimes of flight: $T_1 = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta}{g}$ and $T_2 = \\frac{2v_0\\cos\\theta}{g}$.\nProduct: $T_1T_2 = \\frac{4v_0^2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{g^2} = \\frac{2}{g} \\cdot \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta}{g} = \\frac{2R}{g}$\nSubstituting: $T_1T_2 = \\frac{2 \\times 40}{10}$\nSo $T_1T_2 = 8$ s$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected from level ground with speed 20 m/s at $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$ and neglect air resistance. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Time of flight\n(b) Time at which the vertical component of velocity becomes half its initial value\n(c) Maximum height\n(d) Height of the ball at the instant in (b)\nList-II:\n(i) 0.5 s\n(ii) 2 s\n(iii) 5 m\n(iv) 3.75 m","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{0y} = 20\\sin 30^\\circ = 10$ m/s.\n(a) $T_f = \\frac{2v_{0y}}{g} = \\frac{2 \\times 10}{10} = 2$ s. Match (ii).\n(b) Half of $v_{0y}$ is 5 m/s. From $5 = 10 - 10t$, t = 0.5 s. Match (i).\n(c) $h_m = \\frac{v_{0y}^2}{2g} = \\frac{100}{20} = 5$ m. Match (iii).\n(d) $y = 10(0.5) - 5(0.5)^2 = 3.75$ m. Match (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin with velocity $2\\hat{i}$ m/s and has a constant acceleration $2\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$. Match each quantity in List-I (at t = 3 s) with its value in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $v_x$\n(B) $v_y$\n(C) x-coordinate\n(D) y-coordinate\nList-II:\n(I) 9 m\n(II) 2 m/s\n(III) 6 m\n(IV) 6 m/s","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $a_x = 0$, $v_{0x} = 2$ m/s; $a_y = 2$ m/s$^2$, $v_{0y} = 0$; t = 3 s.\n(A) $v_x = 2$ m/s, constant since $a_x = 0$, so A matches II.\n(B) $v_y = 0 + 2 \\times 3 = 6$ m/s, so B matches IV.\n(C) $x = 2 \\times 3 = 6$ m, so C matches III.\n(D) $y = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 9 = 9$ m, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equation $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$ cannot be used to find the velocity of a particle in uniform circular motion.\nReason (R): In uniform circular motion, the magnitude of the acceleration is constant but its direction keeps changing.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The equation $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$ was derived assuming the acceleration vector is constant.\nIn uniform circular motion, $a_c = v^2/R$ is constant in magnitude, but it always points towards the centre, so its direction keeps changing. R is therefore true.\nBecause the acceleration is not a constant vector, the equation does not apply. A is true.\nR is exactly the reason A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two forces of magnitudes 3 N and 4 N act on a body. Their directions can be chosen freely. Which of the following CANNOT be the magnitude of their resultant?","image":"","options":["1 N","5 N","7 N","8 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: The resultant is greatest when the forces are parallel: $3+4 = 7$ N.\nStep 2: The resultant is least when the forces are antiparallel: $4-3 = 1$ N.\nStep 3: So the resultant must lie in the range $1 \\le R \\le 7$ N. 5 N occurs when the forces are perpendicular.\nStep 4: 8 N exceeds the maximum possible value, so it is impossible.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A vector in a plane can be resolved into components along any two non-collinear vectors lying in that plane.\n(ii) If $\\vec{A}$ makes an angle $\\theta$ with a non-zero vector $\\vec{a}$, then the component vector of $\\vec{A}$ along $\\vec{a}$ in $\\vec{A} = \\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b}$ always has magnitude $A\\cos\\theta$, even when $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are not perpendicular.\n(iii) In $\\vec{r} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j} + z\\hat{k}$, the components of the position vector are the coordinates of the point.\n(iv) A vector of magnitude 5 units can have an x-component of $-5$ units.\n(v) Knowing only the ratio $\\frac{A_y}{A_x}$ fixes the direction of a vector in the x-y plane uniquely.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the parallelogram construction $\\vec{A} = \\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: the magnitude $A\\cos\\theta$ holds only for perpendicular directions. For oblique directions, the parallelogram components differ.\n(iii) Correct: x, y and z are the coordinates of the point.\n(iv) Correct: at $\\theta = 180^\\circ$, $A_x = 5\\cos180^\\circ = -5$.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\tan\\theta$ is the same for $\\theta$ and $\\theta + 180^\\circ$, so the signs of the components are also needed.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin at t = 0 with velocity $6.0\\,\\hat{j}$ m/s. It moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $(2.0\\,\\hat{i} - 1.0\\,\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. What is the y-coordinate of the particle at the instant its x-coordinate is 9.0 m?","image":"","options":["13.5 m","18.0 m","22.5 m","9.0 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{0x} = 0$, $v_{0y} = 6$ m/s, $a_x = 2$ m/s$^2$, $a_y = -1$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: Treat x and y independently: $x = \\frac{1}{2}a_x t^2$ and $y = v_{0y}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_y t^2$.\nSubstituting: $9 = \\frac{1}{2}(2)t^2$, so $t = 3$ s. Then $y = 6(3) - \\frac{1}{2}(1)(9) = 18 - 4.5$.\nSo y = 13.5 m. The tempting 18 m comes from ignoring $a_y$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moving in the x-y plane has an initial velocity $\\vec{v}_0 = 4\\hat{i}$ m/s and a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$. What is its speed at t = 3 s?","image":"","options":["10.0 m/s","7.2 m/s","6.0 m/s","5.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v}_0 = 4\\hat{i}$ m/s, $\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$, t = 3 s.\nFormula: $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$, applied to each component.\nSubstituting: $v_x = 4$ m/s and $v_y = 0 + 2 \\times 3 = 6$ m/s.\nSpeed $= \\sqrt{4^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{52} \\approx 7.2$ m/s.\nThe option 10 m/s comes from adding the magnitudes 4 and 6 directly, which is wrong because the components are perpendicular.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"An object is at point P at time $t$. It moves to point P′ and then comes back to P. Which of the following correctly describes its displacement for the whole trip?","image":"","options":["A vector of magnitude $2\\,PP'$ directed from P to P′","A vector of magnitude $PP'$ directed from P′ to P","A null vector of zero magnitude whose direction cannot be specified","A scalar equal to the total path length $2\\,PP'$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Displacement depends only on the initial and final positions.\nHere the initial and final positions are both P, so the displacement is the null (zero) vector.\nThe magnitude of a null vector is zero, so its direction cannot be specified.\n$2\\,PP'$ is the path length, which is a scalar and is not the displacement. That rules out options (1) and (4).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A small toy car moves with a constant speed of 10 m/s on a circular track of radius 5 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal acceleration?","image":"","options":["2 m/s$^2$","20 m/s$^2$","50 m/s$^2$","0.5 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: v = 10 m/s, R = 5 m.\nFormula: $a_c = \\frac{v^2}{R}$\nSubstituting: $a_c = \\frac{10^2}{5} = \\frac{100}{5}$\nSo $a_c$ = 20 m/s$^2$, directed towards the centre.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along a curved path and is at point P at time t. Its average velocity is found over smaller and smaller time intervals $\\Delta t$ that start at time t. As $\\Delta t \\to 0$, the direction of the average velocity approaches:","image":"","options":["the direction of the position vector of P","the tangent to the path at P, in the direction of motion","the line from P towards the centre of curvature of the path","the chord joining P to the position reached after the first interval"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: average velocity $\\frac{\\Delta\\vec{r}}{\\Delta t}$ always points along the chord $\\Delta\\vec{r}$.\nAs $\\Delta t$ shrinks, the chord swings round. In the limit it lies along the tangent at P, pointing the way the particle moves. So the instantaneous velocity is tangential to the path.\nThe chord option is tempting, but it holds only for a finite interval, not in the limit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle is $\\vec{r} = (t^3\\,\\hat{i} + 2t^2\\,\\hat{j})$ m, where t is in seconds. Match List-I with List-II (all values in SI units).\nList-I\n(a) Speed at t = 1 s\n(b) Magnitude of acceleration at t = 1 s\n(c) x-component of velocity at t = 2 s\n(d) y-component of acceleration at any time\nList-II\n(i) 5\n(ii) $\\sqrt{52}$\n(iii) 12\n(iv) 4","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $v_x = 3t^2$, $v_y = 4t$, $a_x = 6t$, $a_y = 4$.\n(a) At t = 1 s, $v = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$ m/s, so (i).\n(b) At t = 1 s, $a = \\sqrt{6^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{52}$ m/s$^2$, so (ii).\n(c) $v_x = 3(2)^2 = 12$ m/s, so (iii).\n(d) $a_y = 4$ m/s$^2$ at all times, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT's treatment of vectors, a vector that remains unchanged when it is displaced parallel to itself, so that its location does not matter, is called a:","image":"","options":["Localised vector","Null vector","Unit vector","Free vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT: in our study, vectors do not have fixed locations, so displacing a vector parallel to itself leaves it unchanged. Such vectors are called free vectors.\nLocalised vectors are those for which the location or line of application matters, so option (1) is the opposite idea.\nA null vector has zero magnitude, and a unit vector has magnitude 1. Neither term describes this property.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position vector of a particle is $\\vec{r} = (2.0t^2)\\,\\hat{i} + (5.0t)\\,\\hat{j}$ m, where t is in seconds. What is the speed of the particle at t = 3.0 s?","image":"","options":["13 m/s","17 m/s","12 m/s","19.5 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 2.0t^2$ m and $y = 5.0t$ m.\nFormula: $v_x = \\frac{dx}{dt} = 4.0t$, $v_y = \\frac{dy}{dt} = 5.0$, and speed $v = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$.\nSubstituting t = 3.0 s: $v_x = 12$ m/s and $v_y = 5$ m/s, so $v = \\sqrt{144 + 25}$.\nSo the speed is 13 m/s. Adding the components gives the wrong value of 17 m/s, and 12 m/s is only $v_x$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an oblique projectile (air resistance neglected), the velocity and acceleration are perpendicular to each other at the highest point of the path.\nReason (R): At the highest point of its path, the velocity of the projectile becomes zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At the top, $v_y = 0$ but $v_x = v_0\\cos\\theta_0$ is unchanged. So the velocity is horizontal and non-zero.\nThe acceleration $g$ is vertically downward, so the two vectors are perpendicular. A is true.\nR is false. Only the vertical component of velocity vanishes, not the whole velocity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position vector of a particle at t = 0 is $\\vec{r}_1 = (2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j})$ m. At t = 2 s it is $\\vec{r}_2 = (5\\hat{i} - \\hat{j})$ m. The magnitude of the average velocity of the particle over this interval is:","image":"","options":["2.5 m/s","5 m/s","3.5 m/s","1.25 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{r}_1 = 2\\hat{i}+3\\hat{j}$ m, $\\vec{r}_2 = 5\\hat{i}-\\hat{j}$ m, and $\\Delta t$ = 2 s.\nFormula: $\\Delta\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}_2 - \\vec{r}_1$ and $|\\bar{v}| = \\frac{|\\Delta \\vec{r}|}{\\Delta t}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta\\vec{r} = 3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j}$, so $|\\Delta\\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{9+16}$ = 5 m.\nThen $|\\bar{v}|$ = 5/2 = 2.5 m/s. The value 5 m/s is a trap: it forgets to divide by the time.\nSo average velocity = 2.5 m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The instantaneous velocity of a particle moving along a curved path in a plane is directed along the tangent to the path at that point, in the direction of motion.\nStatement II: The average velocity of the particle over a finite time interval is directed along the tangent to the path at the starting point of that interval.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. As $\\Delta t \\to 0$, the displacement $\\Delta \\vec{r}$ lines up with the tangent to the path, so $\\vec{v} = \\frac{d\\vec{r}}{dt}$ is tangential.\nStatement II is incorrect. Average velocity $= \\frac{\\Delta \\vec{r}}{\\Delta t}$ points along the chord joining the initial and final positions. On a curved path this chord is not the tangent at the starting point.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = a_x\\hat{i} + a_y\\hat{j}$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The x-coordinate of the particle at any time depends on the value of $a_y$.","$x = x_0 + v_{0x}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_x t^2$ holds whatever the motion along y may be.","Motion in a plane can be split into two one-dimensional motions only if the acceleration lies along one of the axes.","The y-component of velocity changes only if $a_x$ is non-zero."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: x(t) contains only $x_0$, $v_{0x}$ and $a_x$. It does not involve $a_y$.\n(2) Correct: the x and y motions are independent, so each obeys its own one-dimensional equation.\n(3) Incorrect: any constant acceleration can be resolved along two perpendicular axes, and the motion splits into two independent 1D motions.\n(4) Incorrect: $v_y = v_{0y} + a_y t$, so $v_y$ changes only if $a_y \\neq 0$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is launched from level ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta$ to the horizontal. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Time to reach maximum height\n(B) Time of flight\n(C) Maximum height\n(D) Horizontal range\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta}{g}$\n(ii) $\\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta}{g}$\n(iii) $\\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta)^2}{2g}$\n(iv) $\\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta}{g}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Setting $v_y = 0$ gives $t_m = \\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta}{g}$, which is (ii).\n(B) $T_f = 2t_m = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta}{g}$, which is (iv).\n(C) Substituting $t_m$ into $y$ gives $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta)^2}{2g}$, which is (iii).\n(D) $R = (v_0\\cos\\theta)T_f = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta}{g}$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves from point P to point P′ in a plane along a curved path. The displacement vector of the particle depends on:","image":"","options":["The actual path followed between P and P′","Only the initial position P and the final position P′","The total time taken to go from P to P′","The speed of the particle along the path"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the displacement vector is the straight line joining the initial and final positions.\nIt does not depend on the actual path taken between the two positions. Different paths such as PABCQ, PDQ and PBEFQ all give the same displacement PQ.\nThe path length does depend on the route, which is why option (1) is tempting, but displacement does not.\nTime and speed affect velocity, not displacement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The velocity of a particle moving in a plane is $\\vec{v} = (6\\hat{i} + 8\\hat{j})$ m/s. What is the speed of the particle?","image":"","options":["14 m/s","10 m/s","7.1 m/s","100 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_x = 6$ m/s, $v_y = 8$ m/s.\nFormula: $v = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100}$\nSo the speed is 10 m/s. Simply adding the components to get 14 m/s is wrong, because the components are perpendicular to each other.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors are given as $\\vec{A} = 2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j} - \\hat{k}$ and $\\vec{B} = \\hat{i} - \\hat{j} + 4\\hat{k}$. Their resultant $\\vec{R} = \\vec{A} + \\vec{B}$ is:","image":"","options":["$3\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$","$\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} - 5\\hat{k}$","$3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$","$2\\hat{i} - 3\\hat{j} - 4\\hat{k}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: each component of the resultant is the sum of the matching components, so $R_x = A_x + B_x$, $R_y = A_y + B_y$ and $R_z = A_z + B_z$.\n$R_x = 2 + 1 = 3$\n$R_y = 3 + (-1) = 2$\n$R_z = -1 + 4 = 3$\nSo $\\vec{R} = 3\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$.\nOption (2) is $\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}$. Option (4) multiplies the components instead of adding them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle moving in the x-y plane is $\\vec{r} = 2.0t\\,\\hat{i} + 3.0t^2\\,\\hat{j}$ m, where t is in seconds. What is the magnitude of its acceleration?","image":"","options":["3 m/s^2","2 m/s^2","12 m/s^2","6 m/s^2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{r} = 2.0t\\,\\hat{i} + 3.0t^2\\,\\hat{j}$\nFormula: $\\vec{v} = \\frac{d\\vec{r}}{dt}$ and $\\vec{a} = \\frac{d\\vec{v}}{dt}$\nSubstituting: $\\vec{v} = 2.0\\,\\hat{i} + 6.0t\\,\\hat{j}$, so $\\vec{a} = 6.0\\,\\hat{j}$\nSo the acceleration is 6 m/s^2 along +y. The value 3 m/s^2 comes from forgetting to differentiate $t^2$ correctly.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$, each of magnitude 4 units, make an angle of $60^\\circ$ with each other. What angle does their resultant make with $\\vec{A}$?","image":"","options":["$30^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$20^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = B = 4$, $\\theta = 60^\\circ$.\nFormula: $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{B\\sin\\theta}{A + B\\cos\\theta}$.\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{4(\\sqrt{3}/2)}{4 + 4(1/2)} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{6} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nSo $\\alpha = 30^\\circ$. This is expected: for equal magnitudes the resultant bisects the angle between the vectors.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the null vector $\\vec{0}$ are correct?\n(i) $\\vec{A} + \\vec{0} = \\vec{A}$\n(ii) $\\lambda\\vec{0} = \\vec{0}$ for any real number $\\lambda$\n(iii) $0\\,\\vec{A}$ is the scalar zero, not a vector\n(iv) $\\vec{A} - \\vec{A} = \\vec{0}$ and $|\\vec{0}| = 0$\n(v) The direction of the null vector must always be specified along the x-axis","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: adding the null vector leaves a vector unchanged.\n(ii) Correct: any multiple of the null vector is the null vector.\n(iii) Incorrect: $0\\vec{A} = \\vec{0}$, which is the null vector, not a scalar.\n(iv) Correct: two equal and opposite vectors add to the null vector, which has zero magnitude.\n(v) Incorrect: since its magnitude is zero, its direction cannot be specified.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile launched from level ground at $25^\\circ$ to the horizontal has a certain horizontal range. At what other angle of projection, with the same initial speed, will it have the same range?","image":"","options":["$50^\\circ$","$65^\\circ$","$70^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Range is given by $R = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$.\nAngles $(45^\\circ - \\alpha)$ and $(45^\\circ + \\alpha)$ give equal ranges, because $\\sin(90^\\circ - 2\\alpha) = \\sin(90^\\circ + 2\\alpha)$.\nHere $25^\\circ = 45^\\circ - 20^\\circ$, so the other angle is $45^\\circ + 20^\\circ = 65^\\circ$.\nEquivalently, the two angles are complementary: $90^\\circ - 25^\\circ = 65^\\circ$.\n$45^\\circ$ gives the maximum range, which is a different range.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical balls are projected from the same point on level ground with the same speed $v_0$. One is projected at $15^\\circ$ and the other at $75^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Air resistance is negligible.\nStatement I: Both balls land at the same point on the ground.\nStatement II: If $R$ is their common range and $T_1$, $T_2$ are their times of flight, then $T_1 T_2 = \\frac{2R}{g}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$. Here $\\sin 30^\\circ = \\sin 150^\\circ$, because $15^\\circ$ and $75^\\circ$ are $45^\\circ \\mp 30^\\circ$. So the ranges are equal.\nStatement II is correct. $T_1 T_2 = \\frac{4 v_0^2 \\sin 15^\\circ \\sin 75^\\circ}{g^2} = \\frac{4 v_0^2 \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos 15^\\circ}{g^2} = \\frac{2 v_0^2 \\sin 30^\\circ}{g^2}$.\nThis gives $T_1 T_2 = \\frac{v_0^2}{g^2}$.\nAlso $R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 30^\\circ}{g} = \\frac{v_0^2}{2g}$, so $\\frac{2R}{g} = \\frac{v_0^2}{g^2} = T_1 T_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected from level ground with a speed of 20 m/s at an angle of $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Neglecting air resistance, what is the maximum height reached by the ball? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["2.5 m","5 m","10 m","20 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 20$ m/s, $\\theta_0 = 30^\\circ$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $h_m = \\frac{(v_0 \\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$.\nSubstituting: $v_0 \\sin 30^\\circ = 20 \\times 0.5 = 10$ m/s, so $h_m = \\frac{10^2}{2 \\times 10} = \\frac{100}{20}$.\nSo $h_m = 5$ m. The 20 m option comes from wrongly using the full speed in place of its vertical component; that gives the height for a vertical throw.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The magnitude of a rectangular component of a vector can never exceed the magnitude of the vector itself.\nReason (R): For a vector in the x-y plane, $A = \\sqrt{A_x^2 + A_y^2}$, so $|A_x| \\le A$ and $|A_y| \\le A$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: squaring and adding $A_x = A\\cos\\theta$ and $A_y = A\\sin\\theta$ gives $A^2 = A_x^2 + A_y^2$.\nBecause $A_y^2 \\ge 0$, it follows that $A_x^2 \\le A^2$, so $|A_x| \\le A$. The same argument applies to $A_y$.\nA is therefore true, and it follows directly from R. Equality holds only when the vector lies along that axis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a particle moves in a plane with constant acceleration, its velocity and acceleration vectors must always lie along the same straight line.\nStatement II: Under the same constant acceleration, a particle's trajectory may be a straight line or a parabola, depending on its initial velocity.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. In two or three dimensions, velocity and acceleration can make any angle between 0° and 180°. For example, at the highest point of a projectile's path the velocity is horizontal while the acceleration g points vertically down.\nStatement II is true. The trajectory depends on the initial conditions as well as on the acceleration. Under gravity, a ball dropped from rest falls in a straight line, while a ball thrown at an angle follows a parabola.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin at t = 0 with velocity $5.0\\hat{j}$ m/s. It moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $(4.0\\hat{i} + 2.0\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. What is its y-coordinate at the instant its x-coordinate is 18 m?","image":"","options":["24 m","15 m","9 m","33 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{0x} = 0$, $a_x = 4$ m/s$^2$; $v_{0y} = 5$ m/s, $a_y = 2$ m/s$^2$.\nStep 1, x-motion: $x = \\frac{1}{2}(4)t^2 = 2t^2 = 18$, so t = 3 s.\nStep 2, y-motion: $y = v_{0y}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_y t^2 = 5(3) + \\frac{1}{2}(2)(9)$.\nSubstituting: y = 15 + 9 = 24 m.\nThe options 15 m and 9 m each keep only one of the two terms.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A motorboat heads due north at 30 km/h relative to the water. The water current in that region flows at 15 km/h in the direction $60^\\circ$ east of south. What is the magnitude of the resultant velocity of the boat?","image":"","options":["26.0 km/h","39.7 km/h","33.5 km/h","15.0 km/h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_b = 30$ km/h due north and $v_c = 15$ km/h at $60^\\circ$ east of south.\nThe angle between north and a direction $60^\\circ$ east of south is $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$.\nFormula: $R^2 = v_b^2 + v_c^2 + 2v_b v_c\\cos 120^\\circ$.\nSubstituting: $R^2 = 900 + 225 - 450 = 675$.\nSo $R \\approx 26.0$ km/h.\nUsing $60^\\circ$ as the angle gives 39.7 km/h, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors of magnitudes 6 units and 10 units act at a point with an angle of $60^\\circ$ between them. What is the magnitude of their resultant?","image":"","options":["8.7 units","14 units","16 units","11.7 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: A = 6, B = 10, $\\theta = 60^\\circ$\nFormula (law of cosines): $R = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta}$\nSubstituting: $R = \\sqrt{36 + 100 + 2(6)(10)(0.5)} = \\sqrt{196}$\nSo R = 14 units. The value 8.7 units comes from wrongly using a minus sign in the formula. The value 16 units would only apply if the vectors were parallel.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle in uniform circular motion, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its velocity remains constant.\n(ii) The magnitude of its acceleration remains constant.\n(iii) Its average acceleration over one complete revolution is a null vector.\n(iv) Its velocity is always perpendicular to its position vector drawn from the centre.\n(v) Its acceleration is a constant vector.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the direction of the velocity changes continuously.\n(ii) Correct: $a_c = v^2/R$, and both v and R are constant.\n(iii) Correct: the velocity returns to its initial value after one revolution, so $\\Delta\\mathbf{v} = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: the velocity is tangential, so it is perpendicular to the radius.\n(v) Incorrect: the direction of the acceleration keeps changing.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two forces of magnitudes 5 N and 10 N act at a point. Their resultant is perpendicular to the 5 N force. What is the magnitude of the resultant?","image":"","options":["8.66 N","11.18 N","5.00 N","7.07 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $A = 5$ N, $B = 10$ N, with angle $\\theta$ between them.\nThe resultant is perpendicular to $\\vec{A}$, so in $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{B\\sin\\theta}{A + B\\cos\\theta}$ we need $\\alpha = 90^\\circ$. That requires the denominator to vanish: $A + B\\cos\\theta = 0$.\nThis gives $\\cos\\theta = -\\frac{5}{10} = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so $\\theta = 120^\\circ$.\nThen $R^2 = 25 + 100 + 2(5)(10)(-\\frac{1}{2}) = 75$.\nSo $R = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.66$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Over a given time interval, the magnitude of the average velocity of a particle moving in a plane can be smaller than its average speed.\nReason (R): Average velocity depends only on the initial and final positions, whereas average speed depends on the actual path length covered.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. On a curved or back-and-forth path, the path length exceeds the magnitude of displacement. So average speed (path length / time) is greater than |average velocity| (|displacement| / time).\nR is true. Displacement is fixed by the end points alone, while path length depends on the route taken.\nR is exactly why the two quantities differ, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two vectors of equal magnitude $P$ inclined at $120^\\circ$ to each other have a resultant of magnitude $P$.\nReason (R): The magnitude of the resultant of two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ inclined at angle $\\theta$ is given by $R^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it is the law of cosines derived in NCERT.\nA is true: applying R gives $R^2 = P^2 + P^2 + 2P^2\\cos 120^\\circ$.\nSince $\\cos 120^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{2}$, this becomes $R^2 = 2P^2 - P^2 = P^2$, so $R = P$.\nBecause A follows directly from R, R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The sum 2 kg + 3 m is physically meaningless even though mass and length are both scalars.\nReason (R): Scalars obey the rules of ordinary algebra, so any two scalars can always be added.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Mass and length have different units, so adding them has no meaning.\nR is false. Scalars do follow ordinary algebra, but addition and subtraction make sense only for quantities with the same units.\nMultiplication or division of scalars with different units is allowed. For example, mass ÷ volume gives density.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Any vector $\\vec{A}$ in a plane can be written as $\\vec{A} = \\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b}$, where $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are any two non-zero, non-collinear vectors in that plane and λ, μ are real numbers.\nReason (R): A line drawn through the tail of $\\vec{A}$ parallel to $\\vec{a}$ and a line drawn through its head parallel to $\\vec{b}$ always meet at a point, because $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are not parallel.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. This is exactly the NCERT result for resolving a vector along two non-collinear vectors.\nR is true. Two non-parallel lines in a plane must intersect, say at Q.\nThen $\\vec{OP} = \\vec{OQ} + \\vec{QP}$.\nHere OQ is parallel to $\\vec{a}$, so $\\vec{OQ} = \\lambda\\vec{a}$, and QP is parallel to $\\vec{b}$, so $\\vec{QP} = \\mu\\vec{b}$.\nSo R is precisely the construction that proves A. If $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ were collinear, the lines would never meet and the construction would fail.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The displacement of an object can be zero even when the path length it covers is not zero.\n(ii) The path length can be less than the magnitude of displacement if the object moves very fast.\n(iii) Path length equals the magnitude of displacement only if the object does not change its direction of motion.\n(iv) The displacement vector of an object changes if we shift the origin of the coordinate system.\n(v) The position vector of an object depends on the choice of origin.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. On a round trip the end points coincide, so the displacement is a null vector while the path length is non-zero.\n(ii) Incorrect. Path length is never less than |displacement|, whatever the speed.\n(iii) Correct. This is NCERT's condition for equality.\n(iv) Incorrect. $\\Delta\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}' - \\vec{r}$, and shifting the origin changes both position vectors equally, so the difference is unchanged.\n(v) Correct. The position vector OP is drawn from the chosen origin O.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following algebraic operations with physical quantities is meaningful?","image":"","options":["Adding 3 kg to 2 m","Dividing a distance of 10 m by a time of 2 s","Subtracting 5 s from 8 kg","Adding a scalar to a vector having the same dimensions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that scalars can be added or subtracted only if they have the same units. Scalars with different units can still be multiplied or divided.\nOption (2): 10 m ÷ 2 s = 5 m/s, a meaningful speed.\nOptions (1) and (3) add or subtract quantities with different units, which is meaningless.\nOption (4) is not meaningful because a scalar cannot be added to a vector, even with the same dimensions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is fired from level ground with no air resistance. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its path is described by $y = (\\tan\\theta_0)x - \\frac{g x^2}{2(v_0\\cos\\theta_0)^2}$, which is a parabola.\n(ii) The horizontal component of its velocity stays constant during the flight.\n(iii) The magnitude of its acceleration decreases as it rises and increases as it falls.\n(iv) Its time of flight is twice the time taken to reach maximum height.\n(v) Its speed at the highest point is zero.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the equation has the form $y = ax + bx^2$, which is a parabola.\n(ii) Correct: $a_x = 0$, so $v_x = v_0\\cos\\theta_0$ throughout.\n(iii) Incorrect: the acceleration is always $g$, directed downward.\n(iv) Correct: $T_f = 2t_m$ because the path is symmetric.\n(v) Incorrect: only $v_y$ is zero at the top; the speed there is $v_0\\cos\\theta_0$.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following quantities is completely specified by a single number along with its proper unit?","image":"","options":["Displacement","Mass","Force","Acceleration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A scalar has magnitude only, so one number with its unit specifies it completely.\nMass is a scalar. For example, 2.7 kg is a complete description.\nDisplacement, force and acceleration all need a direction as well. They are vectors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A cricketer can throw a ball with a maximum speed of $30$ m/s. What is the greatest horizontal distance the ball can cover on level ground? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$; neglect air resistance)","image":"","options":["45 m","90 m","180 m","63.6 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 30$ m/s, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $R = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$. This is maximum when $\\sin 2\\theta_0 = 1$, i.e. $\\theta_0 = 45^\\circ$, giving $R_m = \\frac{v_0^2}{g}$.\nSubstituting: $R_m = \\frac{900}{10}$\nSo $R_m = 90$ m.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For two vectors in the x-y plane, the x-component of $\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}$ equals $A_x + B_x$.\nReason (R): Vectors obey the commutative and associative laws of addition, so the terms $A_x\\hat{i}$, $A_y\\hat{j}$, $B_x\\hat{i}$, $B_y\\hat{j}$ can be regrouped along each unit vector.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT shows that $R_x = A_x + B_x$ and $R_y = A_y + B_y$.\nR is true: vector addition is commutative and associative.\nR explains A: these laws let us write $(A_x\\hat{i} + A_y\\hat{j}) + (B_x\\hat{i} + B_y\\hat{j})$ as $(A_x + B_x)\\hat{i} + (A_y + B_y)\\hat{j}$. That regrouping is exactly how A is derived.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected from level ground with a speed of 20 m/s at an angle of $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Neglecting air resistance and taking $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, find the time for which the ball stays in the air.","image":"","options":["1 s","2 s","3.46 s","4 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 20$ m/s, $\\theta_0 = 30^\\circ$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T_f = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\nSubstituting: $T_f = \\frac{2 \\times 20 \\times 0.5}{10}$\nSo the time of flight = 2 s. (1 s is only the time to reach maximum height; 3.46 s comes from wrongly using $\\cos 30^\\circ$.)\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When two vectors in the x-y plane are added analytically, the x-component of the resultant equals the sum of the x-components of the two vectors.\nStatement II: The magnitude of the resultant of two vectors always equals the sum of their magnitudes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT gives $R_x = A_x + B_x$ and $R_y = A_y + B_y$; components add like ordinary numbers.\nStatement II is incorrect. $R = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta}$, which equals $A + B$ only when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$. For any other angle, $R < A + B$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ each have magnitude 5 units. The magnitude of their sum $\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}$ is also 5 units. What is the magnitude of $\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}$?","image":"","options":["5.0 units","7.07 units","10.0 units","8.66 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $A = B = 5$ and $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}| = 5$.\nFormula: $R^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$\nSubstituting: $25 = 25 + 25 + 50\\cos\\theta$, which gives $\\cos\\theta = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so $\\theta = 120^\\circ$.\nNow, $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}|^2 = 25 + 25 - 50\\cos 120^\\circ = 75$.\nSo $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}| = 5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.66$ units.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The temperature of a body can be –10 °C, and temperature is still a scalar.\nStatement II: A scalar quantity must have the same value at every point in space.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A scalar is specified by a number with a unit, and that number can be negative. Temperature and potential energy are examples.\nStatement II is incorrect. A scalar can vary from point to point. The temperature in a room differs near a heater and near a window, but it is still a scalar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the origin of the reference frame is shifted, the position vectors of a moving particle change, but its displacement vector between two given instants does not change.\nStatement II: If, over a certain time interval, the path length of a particle equals the magnitude of its displacement, then the particle must have moved with constant velocity during that interval.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Shifting the origin adds the same constant vector to both $\\vec{r}$ and $\\vec{r}'$. Their difference $\\Delta\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}' - \\vec{r}$ therefore stays the same.\nStatement II is incorrect. Equality only requires that the direction of motion never changes.\nThe particle may still speed up or slow down along the straight line, as a car accelerating along a straight road does. So constant velocity is not necessary.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equations $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v_0} + \\mathbf{a}t$ and $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r_0} + \\mathbf{v_0}t + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}t^2$ cannot be applied to a particle in uniform circular motion.\nReason (R): In uniform circular motion, the magnitude of acceleration is constant but its direction changes continuously.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: these kinematic equations are derived for a constant acceleration vector.\nR is true: centripetal acceleration has the constant magnitude $v^2/R$, but it always points towards the centre, so its direction keeps changing.\nBecause the acceleration is not a constant vector, the equations do not apply. R therefore correctly explains A, as NCERT's Points to Ponder also notes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector $\\vec{A}$ in the x-y plane makes an angle of $210^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise. Which of the following is true about its rectangular components $A_x$ and $A_y$?","image":"","options":["$A_x$ and $A_y$ are both negative","$A_x$ is negative and $A_y$ is positive","$A_x$ is positive and $A_y$ is negative","$A_x$ and $A_y$ are both positive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The components are $A_x = A\\cos\\theta$ and $A_y = A\\sin\\theta$.\nAn angle of $210^\\circ$ lies in the third quadrant.\nThere, $\\cos210^\\circ = -0.866$ and $\\sin210^\\circ = -0.5$, so both components are negative.\nThe tempting option (2) describes the second quadrant, which covers angles between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Rain is falling vertically downward with a speed of 8 m/s. A wind then starts blowing from east to west with a speed of 6 m/s. What is the magnitude of the resultant velocity of the raindrops with respect to the ground?","image":"","options":["10 m/s","14 m/s","2 m/s","12 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_r$ = 8 m/s (vertical) and $v_w$ = 6 m/s (horizontal). The two velocities are perpendicular.\nFormula: $R = \\sqrt{v_r^2 + v_w^2}$\nSubstituting: $R = \\sqrt{64 + 36} = \\sqrt{100}$\nSo R = 10 m/s. Here $\\tan\\theta = 6/8$, so $\\theta \\approx 37^\\circ$ with the vertical. The umbrella should be tilted towards the east.\nOption (2), 14 m/s, is the simple sum; it would hold only if the two velocities were parallel.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle has velocity $(6\\hat{i} + 8\\hat{j})$ m/s at t = 0. It moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $(-\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. What is the minimum speed of the particle during its subsequent motion?","image":"","options":["1.79 m/s","0 m/s","4.0 m/s","10.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_x = 6 - t$ and $v_y = 8 - 2t$ (from $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$).\nFormula: $v^2 = (6 - t)^2 + (8 - 2t)^2$. It is least when $\\frac{d(v^2)}{dt} = 0$, i.e. when $\\vec{v} \\perp \\vec{a}$.\nSubstituting: $-2(6 - t) - 4(8 - 2t) = 0$ gives $10t = 44$, so t = 4.4 s. Then $\\vec{v} = 1.6\\hat{i} - 0.8\\hat{j}$ m/s.\nSo $v_{min} = \\sqrt{2.56 + 0.64} \\approx 1.79$ m/s. Zero is never reached because $v_x$ and $v_y$ do not vanish at the same time.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is thrown with a speed of 30 m/s inside a long hall whose ceiling is 20 m high. The ball is released at floor level. Taking $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, what is the maximum horizontal distance the ball can cover, on returning to floor level, without touching the ceiling?","image":"","options":["89.4 m","90.0 m","80.0 m","67.1 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 30$ m/s, ceiling height $h = 20$ m, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nThe maximum range at $45^\\circ$ would need $h_m = \\frac{v_0^2}{4g} = 22.5$ m, which exceeds the 20 m ceiling. So the ceiling limits the angle.\nSet $h_m = 20$ m: $\\sin^2\\theta = \\frac{2gh}{v_0^2} = \\frac{400}{900}$, so $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$.\nRange: $R = \\frac{2v_0^2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{g} = \\frac{2 \\times 900 \\times 2\\sqrt{5}}{90} = 40\\sqrt{5}$.\nSo $R \\approx$ 89.4 m. (90 m is the $45^\\circ$ value, which would hit the ceiling.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In uniform circular motion the magnitude of acceleration is constant, so the equation $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$ can be used to find the velocity at any later time.\nStatement II: The centripetal acceleration in uniform circular motion is not a constant vector, because its direction keeps changing so that it always points to the centre.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. The magnitude $v^2/R$ is constant, but the direction always points towards the centre, so the acceleration vector keeps changing.\nStatement I is incorrect. The kinematic equations need a constant acceleration vector, in both magnitude and direction.\nNCERT states explicitly that these equations do not apply to uniform circular motion.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"At t = 0 a particle has velocity $6.0\\,\\hat{i}$ m/s. At t = 2.0 s its velocity is $8.0\\,\\hat{j}$ m/s. What is the magnitude of its average acceleration over this interval?","image":"","options":["1 m/s$^2$","5 m/s$^2$","7 m/s$^2$","10 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v}_1 = 6.0\\,\\hat{i}$ m/s, $\\vec{v}_2 = 8.0\\,\\hat{j}$ m/s, and $\\Delta t = 2.0$ s.\nFormula: $\\vec{a}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta \\vec{v}}{\\Delta t}$, where $\\Delta \\vec{v} = -6.0\\,\\hat{i} + 8.0\\,\\hat{j}$.\nSubstituting: $|\\Delta \\vec{v}| = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = 10$ m/s, so $|\\vec{a}_{avg}| = \\frac{10}{2}$.\nSo the average acceleration is 5 m/s$^2$. The value 1 m/s$^2$ wrongly uses only the change in speed, $\\frac{8 - 6}{2}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For an object in uniform circular motion, the acceleration is a constant vector.\nStatement II: For an object in uniform circular motion, the magnitude of the acceleration $\\frac{v^2}{R}$ stays constant.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Incorrect. The acceleration always points towards the centre, so its direction keeps changing as the object moves. A vector whose direction changes is not a constant vector.\nStatement II: Correct. Both v and R are constant, so the magnitude $\\frac{v^2}{R}$ is constant.\nThis is also why the constant-acceleration kinematic equations cannot be used for uniform circular motion.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$, each of magnitude 4 units, are inclined at $120^\\circ$ to each other. What angle does their resultant make with $\\vec{A}$?","image":"","options":["$30^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: A = B = 4, $\\theta = 120^\\circ$.\nFormula: $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{B\\sin\\theta}{A + B\\cos\\theta}$\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{4(0.866)}{4 + 4(-0.5)} = \\frac{3.464}{2} = 1.732$\nSo $\\alpha = 60^\\circ$.\nCheck: for equal vectors the resultant bisects the angle between them, and half of $120^\\circ$ is $60^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Particle X moves with a constant velocity $\\vec{v} = (2\\hat{i} - 3\\hat{j} + 6\\hat{k})$ m/s. Particle Y moves with a constant velocity $-3\\vec{v}$. What is the magnitude of the displacement of particle Y in 2 s?","image":"","options":["42 m","14 m","126 m","21 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v} = 2\\hat{i} - 3\\hat{j} + 6\\hat{k}$ m/s, the velocity of Y is $-3\\vec{v}$, and $t$ = 2 s.\nMagnitude of $\\vec{v}$: $|\\vec{v}| = \\sqrt{4 + 9 + 36} = 7$ m/s.\nSpeed of Y: $|-3\\vec{v}| = 3 \\times 7 = 21$ m/s. The minus sign only reverses the direction.\nDisplacement magnitude: $21 \\times 2 = 42$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle in the x-y plane starts at P with position vector $(2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j})$ m. It moves in a straight line to point A, $(8\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j})$ m. It then moves in a straight line to point Q, $(8\\hat{i} - 5\\hat{j})$ m. The whole trip takes 4 s. What is the ratio of its average speed to the magnitude of its average velocity over the trip?","image":"","options":["1.0","1.4","0.71","2.0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: P(2, 3), A(8, 3), Q(8, −5) in metres; t = 4 s.\nPath length: PA = 6 m and AQ = 8 m, so the total is 14 m.\nDisplacement: $\\Delta \\vec{r} = \\vec{r}_Q - \\vec{r}_P = 6\\hat{i} - 8\\hat{j}$, so its magnitude is $\\sqrt{36+64}$ = 10 m.\nRatio: (14/4) ÷ (10/4) = 14/10. The time cancels.\nSo the ratio = 1.4. It is greater than 1, as expected, since path length ≥ |displacement|.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A stone is thrown horizontally with a speed of $15$ m/s from the edge of a cliff $80$ m high. Neglecting air resistance, with what speed does it strike the ground below? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["42.7 m/s","55 m/s","40 m/s","25 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_x = 15$ m/s, $v_{oy} = 0$, $h = 80$ m.\nTime of fall: $80 = \\frac{1}{2}(10)t^2$, so $t = 4$ s.\nVertical velocity at impact: $v_y = gt = 40$ m/s. The horizontal component remains 15 m/s.\nSpeed: $v = \\sqrt{15^2 + 40^2} = \\sqrt{1825}$\nSo $v \\approx 42.7$ m/s.\n55 m/s comes from adding the components as scalars. 40 m/s ignores the horizontal component.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A car moves with a constant velocity of 4 m/s towards east. The velocity vector is multiplied by a time interval of 5 s. What does the resulting vector represent?","image":"","options":["Displacement of 20 m towards east","Displacement of 1.25 m towards east","Displacement of 0.8 m towards east","Displacement of 9 m towards east"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v}$ = 4 m/s east and $t$ = 5 s, with $t$ a positive scalar.\nFormula: $\\vec{s} = t\\,\\vec{v}$. The dimension of the product is (time)(velocity), which is length.\nSubstituting: $5 \\times 4 = 20$ m. Since $t > 0$, the direction stays east.\nSo the displacement is 20 m towards east.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle is $\\vec{r} = (2t^2\\,\\hat{i} + 5t\\,\\hat{j})$ m, where t is in seconds. What is the magnitude of its average velocity between t = 1 s and t = 3 s?","image":"","options":["9.43 m/s","9.70 m/s","18.9 m/s","6.40 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: at t = 1 s, $\\vec{r} = 2\\hat{i} + 5\\hat{j}$; at t = 3 s, $\\vec{r} = 18\\hat{i} + 15\\hat{j}$.\nFormula: $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{r}}{\\Delta t}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta\\vec{r} = 16\\hat{i} + 10\\hat{j}$ and $\\Delta t = 2$ s, so $\\vec{v}_{avg} = 8\\hat{i} + 5\\hat{j}$ m/s.\nSo its magnitude is $\\sqrt{89} \\approx 9.43$ m/s. Averaging the two instantaneous speeds (6.40 and 13) gives 9.70 m/s, which is not the average velocity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A motorboat heads due north at 20 km/h relative to the water. The water current is 10 km/h in the direction $60^\\circ$ east of south. What is the resultant velocity of the boat with respect to the ground?","image":"","options":["17.3 km/h, $30^\\circ$ east of north","26.5 km/h, $19^\\circ$ east of north","22.4 km/h, $27^\\circ$ east of north","10.0 km/h, $60^\\circ$ east of north"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_b$ = 20 km/h north, $v_c$ = 10 km/h at $60^\\circ$ east of south. The angle between the two vectors is $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$.\nMagnitude: $R = \\sqrt{400 + 100 + 2(20)(10)\\cos120^\\circ} = \\sqrt{300} \\approx 17.3$ km/h\nDirection (law of sines): $\\sin\\phi = \\frac{v_c\\sin120^\\circ}{R} = \\frac{8.66}{17.3} = 0.5$, so $\\phi = 30^\\circ$ east of north.\nCheck with components: east = 8.66 km/h, north = 20 - 5 = 15 km/h, and $\\tan\\phi = \\frac{8.66}{15}$ gives $\\phi = 30^\\circ$.\nOption (2) wrongly uses $\\cos120^\\circ = +0.5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected at an angle $\\theta_0$ ($0^\\circ < \\theta_0 < 90^\\circ$) with the horizontal. Neglect air resistance. At the highest point of its path, what is the angle between the ball's velocity and its acceleration?","image":"","options":["$0^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$180^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At the highest point $v_y = 0$, so the velocity is purely horizontal: $v_x = v_0\\cos\\theta_0$, which is not zero.\nThe acceleration is always $g$, acting vertically downward.\nA horizontal velocity and a vertical acceleration are perpendicular, so the angle is $90^\\circ$.\nThe option $180^\\circ$ would apply only to a ball thrown straight up, while it is rising.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Match each motion in List-I with the ratio (path length) / (magnitude of displacement) in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Moves 6 m along a straight line without reversing\n(B) Moves along a semicircle of radius R, end to end\n(C) Moves along a quarter circle of radius R\n(D) Moves 3 m east and then 4 m north\nList-II\n(P) $\\frac{\\pi}{2\\sqrt{2}}$\n(Q) $\\frac{7}{5}$\n(R) 1\n(S) $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","image":"","options":["A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q","A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) No change of direction, so path length = displacement and the ratio is 1 (R).\n(B) Path length = $\\pi R$ and displacement = 2R, so the ratio is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ (S).\n(C) Path length = $\\frac{\\pi R}{2}$ and the chord = $\\sqrt{2}R$, so the ratio is $\\frac{\\pi}{2\\sqrt{2}}$ (P).\n(D) Path length = 7 m and displacement = $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}$ = 5 m, so the ratio is $\\frac{7}{5}$ (Q).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves uniformly in a circle of radius 0.5 m, completing 2 revolutions every second. Match each quantity in List-I with its value in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Angular speed\n(B) Linear speed\n(C) Centripetal acceleration\n(D) Time period\nList-II\n(i) 0.5 s\n(ii) $2\\pi$ m/s\n(iii) $8\\pi^2$ m/s$^2$\n(iv) $4\\pi$ rad/s","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 2$ rev/s and $R = 0.5$ m.\n(A) $\\omega = 2\\pi\\nu = 4\\pi$ rad/s, which is (iv).\n(B) $v = \\omega R = 4\\pi \\times 0.5 = 2\\pi$ m/s, which is (ii).\n(C) $a_c = \\omega^2 R = 16\\pi^2 \\times 0.5 = 8\\pi^2$ m/s$^2$, which is (iii).\n(D) $T = 1/\\nu = 0.5$ s, which is (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ have magnitudes 3 units and 4 units. How many of the following statements are possible or correct?\n(i) $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}|$ can be 7 units.\n(ii) $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}|$ can be zero.\n(iii) If $\\vec{A} \\perp \\vec{B}$, then $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}| = 5$ units.\n(iv) $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}|$ can be 1 unit.\n(v) If the angle between them is $120^\\circ$, then $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}| = \\sqrt{13}$ units.\n(vi) $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}|$ can be 8 units.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Law of cosines: $R^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$, so $R$ lies between 1 and 7.\n(i) Correct: parallel vectors give 7.\n(ii) Incorrect: the minimum is 1, since the magnitudes are unequal.\n(iii) Correct: $\\sqrt{9+16} = 5$.\n(iv) Correct: parallel vectors give $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}| = 4 - 3 = 1$.\n(v) Correct: $\\sqrt{9+16-12} = \\sqrt{13}$.\n(vi) Incorrect: 8 exceeds the maximum of 7.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two forces of magnitudes 3 N and 5 N act at a point with an angle of $60^\\circ$ between them. What is the magnitude of their resultant?","image":"","options":["8 N","7 N","5.8 N","4.4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: A = 3 N, B = 5 N, $\\theta = 60^\\circ$.\nFormula (law of cosines): $R = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta}$\nSubstituting: $R = \\sqrt{9 + 25 + 2(3)(5)(0.5)} = \\sqrt{49}$\nSo R = 7 N. The value 8 N would apply only if the forces were parallel.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors, each of magnitude A, are added. Match the angle between them (List-I) with the magnitude of the resultant (List-II).\nList-I:\n(P) $0^\\circ$\n(Q) $60^\\circ$\n(R) $90^\\circ$\n(S) $120^\\circ$\nList-II:\n(1) A\n(2) $\\sqrt{2}A$\n(3) $\\sqrt{3}A$\n(4) 2A","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-1, Q-3, R-2, S-4","P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For equal vectors, $R^2 = 2A^2(1 + \\cos\\theta)$.\n(P) $\\theta = 0^\\circ$: $R^2 = 4A^2$, so R = 2A, giving (4).\n(Q) $\\theta = 60^\\circ$: $R^2 = 3A^2$, so R = $\\sqrt{3}A$, giving (3).\n(R) $\\theta = 90^\\circ$: $R^2 = 2A^2$, so R = $\\sqrt{2}A$, giving (2).\n(S) $\\theta = 120^\\circ$: $R^2 = A^2$, so R = A, giving (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Three vectors are given:\n$\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{i} - \\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$\n$\\vec{b} = \\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} - \\hat{k}$\n$\\vec{c} = 3\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$\nThe vector $\\vec{T} = \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} - \\vec{c}$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$","$6\\hat{i} + 5\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k}$","$\\hat{i} + \\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$","$4\\hat{i} - 3\\hat{j} + 5\\hat{k}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: each component of $\\vec{T}$ is found separately as $T_x = a_x + b_x - c_x$, and similarly for $T_y$ and $T_z$.\n$T_x = 2 + 1 - 3 = 0$.\n$T_y = -1 + 4 - 2 = 1$.\n$T_z = 3 - 1 - 1 = 1$.\nSo $\\vec{T} = \\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$.\nOption (2) is $\\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c}$, which adds $\\vec{c}$ instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle moving in the x-y plane is $\\vec{r} = 2t\\,\\hat{i} + 3t^2\\,\\hat{j}$ m, with t in seconds. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its velocity at t = 1 s is $(2\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j})$ m/s.\n(ii) Its acceleration is constant and equal to $6\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$.\n(iii) Its path is a straight line.\n(iv) At t = 0, its velocity and acceleration are perpendicular.\n(v) Its speed at t = 1 s is 8 m/s.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiating, $\\vec{v} = 2\\hat{i} + 6t\\hat{j}$ and $\\vec{a} = 6\\hat{j}$.\n(i) Correct: putting t = 1 s gives $2\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j}$ m/s.\n(ii) Correct: $\\vec{a} = 6\\hat{j}$ m/s$^2$ does not depend on t.\n(iii) Incorrect: with $x = 2t$ and $y = 3t^2$, eliminating t gives $y = \\frac{3x^2}{4}$, a parabola.\n(iv) Correct: at t = 0, $\\vec{v} = 2\\hat{i}$ and $\\vec{a} = 6\\hat{j}$, which are perpendicular.\n(v) Incorrect: speed is $\\sqrt{4+36} = \\sqrt{40} \\approx 6.3$ m/s.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two stones are projected from the same point on level ground, each with speed 20 m/s. One is thrown at $30^\\circ$ and the other at $60^\\circ$ to the horizontal (take g = 10 m/s^2). Their horizontal ranges are equal. What is the ratio of the maximum height of the $60^\\circ$ stone to that of the $30^\\circ$ stone?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{3}$","1","3","1/3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0$ = 20 m/s, angles $60^\\circ$ and $30^\\circ$\nFormula: $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$, so $h_m$ is proportional to $\\sin^2\\theta_0$\nSubstituting: ratio = $\\frac{\\sin^2 60^\\circ}{\\sin^2 30^\\circ} = \\frac{3/4}{1/4}$\nSo the ratio = 3. Equal ranges (angles equally above and below $45^\\circ$) do not mean equal heights. The value $\\sqrt{3}$ comes from forgetting to square the sine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin at t = 0 with velocity $4.0\\,\\hat{j}$ m/s. It moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $2.0\\,\\hat{i}$ m/s^2. At the instant its velocity makes $45^\\circ$ with the x-axis, what is the magnitude of its displacement from the origin?","image":"","options":["5.7 m","12.0 m","6.3 m","8.9 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{0x}=0$, $v_{0y}=4$ m/s, $a_x=2$ m/s^2, $a_y=0$\nCondition: the velocity is at $45^\\circ$ when $v_x = v_y$, i.e. $2t = 4$, so t = 2 s\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{1}{2}(2)(2)^2 = 4$ m and $y = 4 \\times 2 = 8$ m\nSo displacement $= \\sqrt{4^2+8^2} = \\sqrt{80} \\approx 8.9$ m. The value 12.0 m wrongly adds the components directly instead of combining them as perpendicular vectors.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a particle in uniform circular motion of radius R, time period T and frequency $\\nu$.\nList-I:\n(A) Angular speed $\\omega$\n(B) Linear speed v\n(C) Centripetal acceleration $a_c$\n(D) Frequency $\\nu$\nList-II:\n(i) $2\\pi R\\nu$\n(ii) $\\frac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{T}$\n(iv) $\\omega^2 R$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Angular speed is the rate of change of angular displacement, $\\omega = \\Delta\\theta/\\Delta t$, so A-ii.\n(B) The particle covers $2\\pi R$ in time T, so $v = 2\\pi R/T = 2\\pi R\\nu$, giving B-i.\n(C) $a_c = v^2/R = \\omega^2 R$, so C-iv.\n(D) Frequency is the number of revolutions per second, $\\nu = 1/T$, so D-iii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ have magnitudes 3 units and 5 units. They are drawn from a common tail and a parallelogram is completed on them. The diagonal representing $\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}$ has length 7 units. What is the length of the other diagonal, which represents $\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}$?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{19}$ units","8 units","2 units","$\\sqrt{79}$ units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 3, B = 5, $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}|$ = 7.\nFormula: $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}|^2 = A^2 + B^2 - 2AB\\cos\\theta$ and $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}|^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$\nSubstituting: $49 = 34 - 30\\cos\\theta$, which gives $\\cos\\theta = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so $\\theta = 120^\\circ$.\nThen $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}|^2 = 34 - 15 = 19$.\nSo $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}| = \\sqrt{19} \\approx 4.36$ units.\nOption (2), 8 units, would need $\\theta = 0^\\circ$. That is impossible here, because then $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}|$ would be 2, not 7.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A force of magnitude 20 N lies in the x-y plane. It makes an angle of $150^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise. What is its x-component $F_x$?","image":"","options":["17.3 N","−17.3 N","−10 N","10 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: F = 20 N, $\\theta = 150^\\circ$ with the +x axis.\nFormula: $F_x = F\\cos\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $F_x = 20\\cos150^\\circ = 20 \\times (-0.866) = -17.3$ N.\nThe sign is negative because the vector points into the second quadrant. A component can be positive, negative or zero depending on θ.\n10 N is the magnitude of $F_y = 20\\sin150^\\circ$, not $F_x$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Electric current in a wire is treated as a scalar quantity even though we speak of the direction in which it flows.\nReason (R): A physical quantity is a vector only if, besides having magnitude and direction, it also obeys the triangle law (or parallelogram law) of addition.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT defines a vector as a quantity that has both magnitude and direction and also obeys the triangle (or parallelogram) law of addition.\nA is true. Two currents of 3 A and 4 A meeting at a junction at 90° give 7 A in the outgoing wire, not 5 A. They add like ordinary numbers, not like vectors.\nCurrent therefore fails the addition test, so it is a scalar despite having a direction.\nR is the reason behind A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along an arbitrary path in space. Its acceleration may vary in both magnitude and direction. How many of the following relations are valid in general?\n(i) $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{\\vec{r}(t_2) - \\vec{r}(t_1)}{t_2 - t_1}$\n(ii) $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{1}{2}[\\vec{v}(t_1) + \\vec{v}(t_2)]$\n(iii) $\\vec{a}_{avg} = \\frac{\\vec{v}(t_2) - \\vec{v}(t_1)}{t_2 - t_1}$\n(iv) $\\vec{r}(t) = \\vec{r}(0) + \\vec{v}(0)t + \\frac{1}{2}\\vec{a}t^2$\n(v) $\\vec{v} = \\frac{d\\vec{r}}{dt}$\n(vi) $\\vec{v}(t) = \\vec{v}(0) + \\vec{a}t$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Valid: this is the definition of average velocity.\n(ii) Not valid: it holds only for constant acceleration.\n(iii) Valid: this is the definition of average acceleration.\n(iv) Not valid: it requires constant $\\vec{a}$.\n(v) Valid: this is the definition of instantaneous velocity.\n(vi) Not valid: it requires constant $\\vec{a}$.\nSo three relations, (i), (iii) and (v), are valid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Let $\\hat{n}$ be the unit vector along a non-zero vector $\\vec{A}$.\nStatement I: The unit vector along $-3\\vec{A}$ is $-\\hat{n}$.\nStatement II: For any non-zero real number $\\lambda$, the unit vector along $\\lambda\\vec{A}$ is $\\lambda\\hat{n}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: $-3\\vec{A} = -3|\\vec{A}|\\hat{n}$. Dividing by its magnitude $3|\\vec{A}|$ gives $-\\hat{n}$. Correct.\nStatement II: $\\lambda\\hat{n}$ has magnitude $|\\lambda|$, which is not 1 unless $|\\lambda| = 1$, so it is not a unit vector. The correct result is $\\pm\\hat{n}$, depending on the sign of $\\lambda$. Incorrect.\nA unit vector depends only on direction, not on magnitude.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about motion in a plane are correct?\n(i) The average velocity over an interval has the same direction as the displacement over that interval.\n(ii) The x-component of velocity can be zero at an instant when the speed is not zero.\n(iii) If x is known as a function of time, then $v_x = \\frac{dx}{dt}$.\n(iv) If the speed of a particle stays constant, its acceleration must be zero.\n(v) The magnitude of the x-component of velocity can be greater than the speed.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{r}}{\\Delta t}$ and $\\Delta t > 0$, so it points along $\\Delta\\vec{r}$.\n(ii) Correct: the motion may be purely along y at that instant, as at the top of a vertical throw component.\n(iii) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of $v_x$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the direction of velocity can change, as in uniform circular motion, so acceleration need not be zero.\n(v) Incorrect: $v = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2} \\ge |v_x|$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For two non-zero vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$, $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}| = |\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}|$ holds only if the two vectors are perpendicular to each other.\nStatement II: Over any time interval, the average speed of a moving object is always strictly greater than the magnitude of its average velocity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. By the law of cosines, $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}|^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB\\cos\\theta$. Since $-\\vec{B}$ makes angle $180^\\circ - \\theta$ with $\\vec{A}$, $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}|^2 = A^2 + B^2 - 2AB\\cos\\theta$. These are equal only if $\\cos\\theta = 0$, that is, $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.\nStatement II is incorrect. Average speed is greater than or equal to the magnitude of average velocity. The two are equal when the object moves without changing direction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A boy walks along a semicircular track of radius 5 m, from one end of a diameter to the other end. What is the magnitude of his displacement? (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)","image":"","options":["10 m","15.7 m","5 m","31.4 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: radius R = 5 m. The boy goes from one end of a diameter to the other.\nFormula: the displacement is the straight line joining the initial and final points. Here that line is the diameter, so the magnitude is 2R.\nSubstituting: |displacement| = 2 × 5 = 10 m.\nThe value 15.7 m is the path length, $\\pi R$, not the displacement.\nSo displacement = 10 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A child runs along a semicircular track of radius 7 m, from one end P of a diameter to the other end Q. What is the magnitude of the child's displacement? (Take $\\pi = \\frac{22}{7}$)","image":"","options":["22 m","14 m","7 m","44 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: radius R = 7 m. P and Q are the two ends of a diameter.\nFormula: displacement is the straight line from the initial to the final position, whatever path is taken. So its magnitude = PQ = 2R.\nSubstituting: PQ = 2 × 7 = 14 m. The path length would be $\\pi R$ = 22 m, which is the tempting wrong option.\nSo magnitude of displacement = 14 m.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ are added by the head-to-tail (triangle) method. The tail of $\\vec{B}$ is placed at the head of $\\vec{A}$. The resultant $\\vec{R} = \\vec{A} + \\vec{B}$ is represented by the line drawn from","image":"","options":["the head of $\\vec{A}$ to the tail of $\\vec{B}$","the tail of $\\vec{A}$ to the head of $\\vec{B}$","the head of $\\vec{B}$ to the tail of $\\vec{A}$","the tail of $\\vec{B}$ to the head of $\\vec{A}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: In the head-to-tail method, $\\vec{B}$ is shifted parallel to itself so that its tail sits at the head of $\\vec{A}$.\nStep 2: The resultant joins the free tail of $\\vec{A}$ to the free head of $\\vec{B}$. Its arrow points towards the head of $\\vec{B}$.\nStep 3: Option (3) uses the same line but reverses the arrow. That arrow represents $-(\\vec{A}+\\vec{B})$, not the sum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equation $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$ cannot be used to find the velocity of a particle in uniform circular motion.\nReason (R): In uniform circular motion, the magnitude of the acceleration keeps changing as the particle goes round the circle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: The kinematic equations hold only when the acceleration vector is constant. In uniform circular motion, the acceleration always points towards the centre, so its direction changes continuously. The equations therefore do not apply, and A is true.\nReason: The magnitude of the acceleration is $v^2/R$. Since v and R are both constant, this magnitude stays constant. Only the direction changes, so R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector $\\vec{A}$ has magnitude 4 m and points due east. What is the vector $-1.5\\vec{A}$?","image":"","options":["6 m, due west","6 m, due east","2.5 m, due west","4 m, due west"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Multiplying a vector by a negative number $-\\lambda$ reverses its direction. It also scales the magnitude by $\\lambda$.\nMagnitude: $1.5 \\times 4 = 6$ m.\nDirection: opposite to east, so the vector points due west.\n'6 m, due east' is wrong because it ignores the minus sign. '2.5 m' wrongly adds $-1.5$ to 4 instead of multiplying. '4 m, due west' reverses the direction but does not scale the magnitude.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is thrown horizontally with a speed of 20 m/s from the top of a cliff 80 m high. Neglecting air resistance and taking $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, find the speed with which the ball strikes the ground.","image":"","options":["40 m/s","44.7 m/s","60 m/s","20 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_x = 20$ m/s, $v_{0y} = 0$, $h = 80$ m, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nTime of fall: $h = \\frac{1}{2}gt^2$, so $80 = 5t^2$, giving $t = 4$ s.\nVertical velocity at impact: $v_y = gt = 40$ m/s; $v_x$ stays 20 m/s.\nSo the speed $= \\sqrt{20^2 + 40^2} = \\sqrt{2000} \\approx$ 44.7 m/s. (40 m/s ignores the horizontal component.)\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is thrown twice from level ground with the same speed of 20 m/s. The first throw is at $30^\\circ$ and the second at $60^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Both throws land at the same horizontal distance. Neglect air resistance and take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$. What is the sum of the maximum heights reached in the two throws?","image":"","options":["10 m","15 m","20 m","40 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 20$ m/s, angles $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$ (these give equal ranges), $g = 10\\ m/s^2$.\nFormula: $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$.\nSubstituting: $h_{30} = \\frac{400 \\times 0.25}{20} = 5$ m and $h_{60} = \\frac{400 \\times 0.75}{20} = 15$ m.\nSo the sum is $5 + 15 = 20$ m. This equals $\\frac{v_0^2}{2g}$, because $\\sin^2 30^\\circ + \\sin^2 60^\\circ = 1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle is $\\vec{r} = [(t^2 - 4t)\\,\\hat{i} + 3t\\,\\hat{j}]$ m, where t is in seconds. At t = 1 s, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["The angle between velocity and acceleration is about 124°, and the speed is decreasing","The angle between velocity and acceleration is about 56°, and the speed is increasing","The angle between velocity and acceleration is about 124°, and the speed is increasing","Velocity and acceleration are perpendicular, and the speed is momentarily constant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $\\vec{v} = (2t - 4)\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}$ and $\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{i}$ (constant).\nAt t = 1 s, $\\vec{v} = -2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}$, so $v = \\sqrt{13}$ m/s.\nAngle: $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\vec{v} \\cdot \\vec{a}}{va} = \\frac{-4}{2\\sqrt{13}} \\approx -0.555$, so $\\theta \\approx 124°$.\nSpeed: $v^2 = (2t - 4)^2 + 9$ is falling at t = 1 s. This fits the angle being greater than 90°: the component of a along v opposes the motion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about unit vectors is correct?","image":"","options":["A unit vector along a velocity has magnitude 1 m/s.","The magnitudes of $\\hat{i}$, $\\hat{j}$ and $\\hat{k}$ depend on the unit chosen for length.","The unit vector along $\\vec{A}$ is $\\hat{n} = \\vec{A}\\,|\\vec{A}|$.","A unit vector has no dimension and no unit; it only specifies a direction."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: a unit vector has pure magnitude 1 with no unit attached.\n(2) Incorrect: $|\\hat{i}| = |\\hat{j}| = |\\hat{k}| = 1$, whatever units are used.\n(3) Incorrect: the correct relation is $\\hat{n} = \\vec{A}/|\\vec{A}|$, which follows from $\\vec{A} = |\\vec{A}|\\hat{n}$.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states that a unit vector is dimensionless, has no unit and serves only to specify a direction.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The trajectory of a projectile launched from the origin is $y = x - \\frac{x^2}{40}$, where $x$ and $y$ are in metres, the x-axis is horizontal and the y-axis is vertically upward. What is the maximum height reached by the projectile? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["10 m","20 m","40 m","5 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compare with $y = (\\tan\\theta_0)x - \\frac{g x^2}{2v_0^2\\cos^2\\theta_0}$.\nFrom the $x$ term: $\\tan\\theta_0 = 1$, so $\\theta_0 = 45^\\circ$.\nFrom the $x^2$ term: $\\frac{10}{2v_0^2(0.5)} = \\frac{1}{40}$, so $v_0^2 = 400$.\nFormula: $h_m = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g} = \\frac{400 \\times 0.5}{20}$\nSo $h_m = 10$ m. (Check: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$ at $x = 20$ gives $y = 20 - 10 = 10$ m.)\n40 m is the range, not the height.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ are drawn from a common origin O, and the parallelogram is completed.\nStatement I: The diagonal drawn from O to the opposite corner of the parallelogram represents $\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}$.\nStatement II: The other diagonal, directed from the head of $\\vec{A}$ to the head of $\\vec{B}$, represents $\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the parallelogram method, the resultant runs from the common origin along the diagonal.\nStatement II is incorrect. Going from the head of $\\vec{A}$ to the head of $\\vec{B}$ means that $\\vec{A}$ plus this vector equals $\\vec{B}$. So this vector is $\\vec{B}-\\vec{A}$.\nThe vector $\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}$ is the same diagonal taken in the opposite direction, from the head of $\\vec{B}$ to the head of $\\vec{A}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The vector $\\vec{A} = 7\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}$ (in m) is resolved into two component vectors $\\lambda\\vec{a}$ and $\\mu\\vec{b}$. These lie along $\\vec{a} = \\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j}$ and $\\vec{b} = 2\\hat{i} - \\hat{j}$, so that $\\vec{A} = \\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b}$. What is the magnitude of the component vector along $\\vec{b}$?","image":"","options":["6.71 m","2.0 m","4.47 m","8.06 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{A} = \\lambda(\\hat{i}+2\\hat{j}) + \\mu(2\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$.\nComparing components: $\\lambda + 2\\mu = 7$ and $2\\lambda - \\mu = 4$.\nSolving these gives $\\lambda = 3$ and $\\mu = 2$.\nThe component vector along $\\vec{b}$ is $\\mu\\vec{b} = 4\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j}$, so its magnitude is $\\sqrt{16+4} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\approx 4.47$ m.\nTraps: 2.0 is just μ, 6.71 m is $|\\lambda\\vec{a}|$, and 8.06 m is $|\\vec{A}|$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For two non-zero vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$, $|\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}| = |\\vec{a}| + |\\vec{b}|$ holds only when $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ point in the same direction.\nReason (R): In the triangle method, $\\vec{a}$, $\\vec{b}$ and $\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}$ form three sides of a triangle. One side of a triangle is shorter than the sum of the other two, and the triangle flattens into a straight line only when $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are parallel.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The magnitude of the sum equals the sum of the magnitudes only when the vectors are parallel, that is, along the same direction.\nR is true. The head-to-tail construction gives a triangle, in which $|\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}| < |\\vec{a}|+|\\vec{b}|$. Equality appears only when the triangle collapses into a line, which happens when $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are parallel.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about any two non-zero vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are correct?\n(i) $|\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}| \\le |\\vec{a}|+|\\vec{b}|$\n(ii) $|\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}| \\ge \\big||\\vec{a}|-|\\vec{b}|\\big|$\n(iii) $|\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}|$ is always greater than $|\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}|$\n(iv) $\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}$ can be found graphically by placing $-\\vec{b}$ head-to-tail with $\\vec{a}$\n(v) If $|\\vec{a}+\\vec{b}| = |\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}|$, then $\\vec{a}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{b}$","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\vec{a}$, $-\\vec{b}$ and $\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}$ form a triangle, so the triangle inequality applies.\n(ii) Correct: one side of a triangle is at least the difference of the other two.\n(iii) Incorrect: for an angle greater than $90^\\circ$, the inequality reverses.\n(iv) Correct: this follows from $\\vec{a}-\\vec{b}=\\vec{a}+(-\\vec{b})$.\n(v) Correct: by the law of cosines, $2ab\\cos\\theta = -2ab\\cos\\theta$, so $\\cos\\theta = 0$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is launched from the ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta_0$ above the horizontal. Air resistance is negligible.\nStatement I: At the highest point of its path, the speed of the projectile is $v_0\\cos\\theta_0$.\nStatement II: At the highest point of its path, the acceleration of the projectile is zero.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. At the top, $v_y = 0$, and $v_x = v_0\\cos\\theta_0$ stays constant throughout the flight. So the speed there equals $v_0\\cos\\theta_0$.\nStatement II is incorrect. The acceleration is $\\mathbf{a} = -g\\hat{j}$ at every instant, including at the highest point. Only the vertical velocity is zero there.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected from level ground with a speed of 20 m/s at $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ and neglect air resistance. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its time of flight is 2 s.\n(ii) Its maximum height is 5 m.\n(iii) Its horizontal range is about 34.6 m.\n(iv) Its speed at the highest point is 10 m/s.\n(v) Its acceleration at the highest point is zero.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $T_f = 2(20)(0.5)/10 = 2$ s.\n(ii) Correct: $h_m = (10)^2/20 = 5$ m.\n(iii) Correct: $R = 400\\sin60^\\circ/10 \\approx 34.6$ m.\n(iv) Incorrect: at the top only $v_x$ remains, so the speed is $20\\cos30^\\circ \\approx 17.3$ m/s.\n(v) Incorrect: the acceleration is $g$ downward throughout the flight.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Vector $\\vec{A}$ has magnitude 10 units and a fixed direction. Vector $\\vec{B}$ has magnitude 6 units and may point in any direction in the same plane. Using the triangle method, find the maximum possible angle between the resultant $\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}$ and $\\vec{A}$.","image":"","options":["$53.1^\\circ$","$36.9^\\circ$","$31.0^\\circ$","$59.0^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Place $\\vec{B}$ head-to-tail on $\\vec{A}$. As the direction of $\\vec{B}$ changes, its head traces a circle of radius 6 centred at the head of $\\vec{A}$.\nStep 2: The resultant runs from the tail of $\\vec{A}$ to a point on this circle. Its angle with $\\vec{A}$ is greatest when it is tangent to the circle.\nStep 3: At tangency, $\\vec{B}$ is perpendicular to the resultant, so $\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{B}{A} = 0.6$.\nSo $\\alpha \\approx 36.9^\\circ$. The tempting $53.1^\\circ$ wrongly uses $\\cos\\alpha = 0.6$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is thrown with a speed of $30$ m/s inside a long hall whose ceiling is $15$ m above the point of projection. What is the greatest horizontal distance the ball can travel, landing back at the level of projection, without touching the ceiling? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$; neglect the ball's size)","image":"","options":["84.9 m","90 m","60 m","42.4 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 30$ m/s, height limit $h_m = 15$ m.\nUnrestricted, the ball would go farthest at $45^\\circ$, but then $h_m = 22.5$ m, which exceeds 15 m. So the greatest range occurs when $h_m = 15$ m exactly.\nSetting $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g} = 15$: $\\sin^2\\theta_0 = \\frac{300}{900} = \\frac{1}{3}$, so $\\sin\\theta_0 = 0.577$ and $\\cos\\theta_0 = 0.816$.\nRange: $R = \\frac{2v_0^2\\sin\\theta_0\\cos\\theta_0}{g} = \\frac{2 \\times 900 \\times 0.471}{10}$\nSo $R \\approx 84.9$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Rain is falling vertically at $20\\sqrt{3}$ m/s. A wind starts blowing from east to west at 20 m/s. How should a person standing still hold an umbrella for best protection?","image":"","options":["At $60^\\circ$ with the vertical, towards the west","At $30^\\circ$ with the vertical, towards the west","At $60^\\circ$ with the vertical, towards the east","At $30^\\circ$ with the vertical, towards the east"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $v_r = 20\\sqrt{3}$ m/s (vertically down) and $v_w = 20$ m/s (towards the west).\nFormula: angle of the resultant with the vertical, $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{v_w}{v_r}$\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{20}{20\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so $\\theta = 30^\\circ$.\nThe rain moves downward and westward, so it arrives from the east. The umbrella must be tilted $30^\\circ$ from the vertical towards the east, as in the NCERT example.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"For a projectile launched from level ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta_0$ (no air resistance), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) Time to reach maximum height\n(Q) Time of flight\n(R) Maximum height\n(S) Horizontal range\nList-II:\n(1) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$\n(2) $\\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(3) $\\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(4) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g}$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1","P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) At the top, $v_y = 0$, so $t_m = \\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$: matches (3).\n(Q) $T_f = 2t_m = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$: matches (2).\n(R) Substituting $t_m$ into $y$ gives $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$: matches (4).\n(S) $R = (v_0\\cos\\theta_0)T_f = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$: matches (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Given $\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{i} + \\hat{j} - 3\\hat{k}$, $\\vec{b} = -\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j} + 2\\hat{k}$ and $\\vec{c} = 3\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$, the vector $\\vec{T} = \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} - \\vec{c}$ is:","image":"","options":["$4\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j} + 0\\hat{k}$","$-2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j} - 4\\hat{k}$","$-2\\hat{i} + 7\\hat{j} - 2\\hat{k}$","$2\\hat{i} + 7\\hat{j} - 2\\hat{k}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: $T_x = a_x + b_x - c_x$, and similarly for the y and z components.\n$T_x = 2 - 1 - 3 = -2$\n$T_y = 1 + 4 - (-2) = 7$\n$T_z = -3 + 2 - 1 = -2$\nSo $\\vec{T} = -2\\hat{i} + 7\\hat{j} - 2\\hat{k}$.\nOption (1) is $\\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c}$. Option (2) comes from forgetting that subtracting $-2$ means adding 2 in the y-component.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is projected from level ground with a speed of $20$ m/s at an angle of $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Neglecting air resistance, how long does the ball stay in the air? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["1 s","2 s","4 s","3.46 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 20$ m/s, $\\theta_0 = 30^\\circ$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T_f = \\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\nSubstituting: $T_f = \\frac{2 \\times 20 \\times 0.5}{10}$\nSo $T_f = 2$ s.\n1 s is only the time to reach maximum height, $t_m$. 3.46 s comes from wrongly using $\\cos 30^\\circ$ instead of $\\sin 30^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is launched from level ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta_0$ above the horizontal. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Time to reach maximum height\n(b) Time of flight\n(c) Maximum height\n(d) Horizontal range\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(ii) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$\n(iii) $\\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(iv) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) At the top $v_y = 0$, so $v_0\\sin\\theta_0 - gt_m = 0$, giving $t_m = \\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$. Match (iii).\n(b) The parabolic path is symmetric, so $T_f = 2t_m$. Match (i).\n(c) Putting $t_m$ into $y$ gives $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$. Match (iv).\n(d) $R = (v_0\\cos\\theta_0)T_f = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$. Match (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a projectile moving under gravity (air resistance neglected), the acceleration is $g$, directed vertically downward, at every point of the path, including the highest point.\nStatement II: At the highest point of its path, the velocity of the projectile is zero.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. After launch, the only acceleration is due to gravity, so $a_x = 0$ and $a_y = -g$ throughout the flight.\nStatement II: Incorrect. At the top only $v_y = 0$. The horizontal component $v_0\\cos\\theta_0$ is still present, so the velocity is not zero.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moving in the x-y plane has initial velocity $\\vec{v}_0 = (6\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m/s and a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = (-1\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j})\\ m/s^2$. What is the minimum speed of the particle during its motion?","image":"","options":["2.68 m/s","3.58 m/s","4.47 m/s","7.21 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{v}_0 = 6\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}$, $\\vec{a} = -\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j}$.\nFormula: $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$, which gives $v_x = 6 - t$ and $v_y = 4 - 2t$.\nSpeed is minimum when $v^2 = (6-t)^2 + (4-2t)^2$ is minimum. Setting the derivative to zero: $-2(6-t) - 4(4-2t) = 0$, so $t = 2.8$ s. At this instant, $\\vec{v} \\perp \\vec{a}$.\nSubstituting: $v_x = 3.2$ m/s and $v_y = -1.6$ m/s, so $v = \\sqrt{10.24 + 2.56} = \\sqrt{12.8}$.\nSo the minimum speed is about 3.58 m/s. The value 7.21 m/s is only the initial speed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"For the vector $\\vec{A} = 6\\hat{i} - 8\\hat{j}$, which of the following is a vector of magnitude 5 in the direction of $\\vec{A}$?","image":"","options":["$3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j}$","$-3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}$","$0.6\\hat{i} - 0.8\\hat{j}$","$30\\hat{i} - 40\\hat{j}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Magnitude: $|\\vec{A}| = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = 10$.\nUnit vector: $\\hat{n} = \\frac{\\vec{A}}{|\\vec{A}|} = 0.6\\hat{i} - 0.8\\hat{j}$.\nRequired vector: $5\\hat{n} = 3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j}$.\nOption (3) is only the unit vector, with magnitude 1. Option (2) has magnitude 5 but points opposite to $\\vec{A}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position vector of a particle is $\\vec{r} = (3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j} + 12\\hat{k})$ m. Here $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$ are the angles $\\vec{r}$ makes with the x-, y- and z-axes respectively. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) $|\\vec{r}|$ (in m)\n(b) $\\cos\\alpha$\n(c) $\\cos\\beta$\n(d) z-coordinate of the particle (in m)\nList-II:\n(i) 12\n(ii) 13\n(iii) $-\\frac{4}{13}$\n(iv) $\\frac{3}{13}$","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii","a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) $|\\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{9 + 16 + 144} = 13$ m, which matches (ii).\n(b) $\\cos\\alpha = x/r = 3/13$, which matches (iv).\n(c) $\\cos\\beta = y/r = -4/13$, which matches (iii). It is negative because the y-coordinate is negative.\n(d) The components of $\\vec{r}$ are the coordinates of the particle, so z = 12 m, which matches (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle starts from the origin with velocity $(6\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j})$ m/s and moves with a constant acceleration $(-3\\hat{i} + 1\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. How far is it from the origin at the instant its x-component of velocity becomes zero?","image":"","options":["8.49 m","6.0 m","7.21 m","10.0 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{0x} = 6$ m/s, $a_x = -3$ m/s$^2$; $v_{0y} = 2$ m/s, $a_y = 1$ m/s$^2$.\nStep 1, time: $v_x = 6 - 3t = 0$, so t = 2 s.\nStep 2, x-coordinate: $x = 6(2) - \\frac{1}{2}(3)(4) = 12 - 6 = 6$ m.\nStep 3, y-coordinate: $y = 2(2) + \\frac{1}{2}(1)(4) = 4 + 2 = 6$ m.\nSo distance $= \\sqrt{6^2 + 6^2} = 6\\sqrt{2} \\approx 8.49$ m.\nThe option 6.0 m gives only x or only y.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves in the x-y plane with a constant acceleration $\\vec{a} = a_y\\hat{j}$, where $a_y \\neq 0$.\nStatement I: The x-component of the particle's velocity stays constant throughout the motion.\nStatement II: The relation $\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}_0 + \\vec{v}_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}\\vec{a}t^2$ holds only if one of the coordinate axes is chosen along the acceleration.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Since $a_x = 0$, the component equation $v_x = v_{0x} + a_x t$ gives $v_x = v_{0x}$ at all times.\nStatement II is incorrect. The vector equations $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$ and $\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}_0 + \\vec{v}_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}\\vec{a}t^2$ do not depend on any choice of axes. They can be resolved along any two independent axes. Choosing an axis along $\\vec{a}$ only makes the algebra easier.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following physical quantities are vectors?\ndisplacement, speed, acceleration, mass, velocity, temperature, force","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A vector has both magnitude and direction and obeys the triangle law of addition.\nVectors: displacement, acceleration, velocity and force, which makes 4.\nScalars: speed, mass and temperature. Each is fully specified by a number with its unit.\nNote that speed is the magnitude of velocity, so it is a scalar.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform solid cube of side 5 cm has a mass of 0.9 kg. What is the density of the material of the cube?","image":"","options":["$7.2 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$1.8 \\times 10^{2}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$7.2 \\times 10^{2}$ kg m$^{-3}$","$7.2 \\times 10^{4}$ kg m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: side a = 5 cm = 0.05 m, mass m = 0.9 kg.\nFormula: volume $V = a^3$ and density $\\rho = \\frac{m}{V}$. Both are scalars and combine by ordinary algebra.\nSubstituting: $V = (0.05)^3 = 1.25 \\times 10^{-4}$ m$^3$, so $\\rho = \\frac{0.9}{1.25 \\times 10^{-4}}$.\nSo density = $7.2 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves with a constant speed of 10 m/s on a circle of radius 5 m. It starts at one end of a diameter and travels along the circle to the opposite end. What is the magnitude of its average acceleration over this interval?","image":"","options":["12.73 m/s$^2$","18.01 m/s$^2$","20.0 m/s$^2$","0 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: v = 10 m/s, R = 5 m, half a revolution.\nChange in velocity: At the opposite end of the diameter, the velocity is reversed. So $|\\Delta \\vec{v}| = 2v = 20$ m/s.\nTime taken: $t = \\frac{\\pi R}{v} = \\frac{5\\pi}{10} \\approx 1.571$ s.\nAverage acceleration: $|\\vec{a}_{avg}| = \\frac{|\\Delta \\vec{v}|}{t} = \\frac{20}{1.571} \\approx 12.73$ m/s$^2$.\nWhy the others fail: 20 m/s$^2$ is the instantaneous value $v^2/R$. 18.01 m/s$^2$ is the average over a quarter revolution. 0 m/s$^2$ is the average over a full cycle.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two vectors each of magnitude $A$ are inclined at an angle $\\theta$. Match the angle in List-I with the magnitude of the resultant in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $\\theta = 0^\\circ$\n(b) $\\theta = 90^\\circ$\n(c) $\\theta = 120^\\circ$\n(d) $\\theta = 180^\\circ$\nList-II\n(p) $A$\n(q) zero\n(r) $2A$\n(s) $\\sqrt{2}A$","image":"","options":["a-r, b-s, c-p, d-q","a-r, b-p, c-s, d-q","a-s, b-r, c-p, d-q","a-r, b-s, c-q, d-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For equal magnitudes, $R^2 = 2A^2(1 + \\cos\\theta)$.\n(a) $\\cos 0^\\circ = 1$, so $R = 2A$ → (r).\n(b) $\\cos 90^\\circ = 0$, so $R = \\sqrt{2}A$ → (s).\n(c) $\\cos 120^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so $R = A$ → (p).\n(d) $\\cos 180^\\circ = -1$, so $R = 0$, the null vector → (q).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (motion) with List-II (angle between velocity and acceleration at the stated instant):\nList-I\n(A) Particle in uniform circular motion\n(B) Ball thrown vertically upward, while rising\n(C) Stone dropped from rest, while falling\n(D) Projectile just after launch at $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal\nList-II\n(I) $0^\\circ$\n(II) $90^\\circ$\n(III) $120^\\circ$\n(IV) $180^\\circ$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) In uniform circular motion, velocity is tangential and acceleration points towards the centre. They are perpendicular: II.\n(B) While rising, velocity is up and $g$ is down. They are opposite: IV.\n(C) While falling, both velocity and $g$ point down: I.\n(D) Velocity is $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal and $g$ points straight down. The angle between them is $30^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 120^\\circ$: III.\nThis matches NCERT's remark that in a plane, the angle between velocity and acceleration can be anything from $0^\\circ$ to $180^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, a thorough analysis of centripetal acceleration was first published in 1673 by which scientist?","image":"","options":["Isaac Newton","Christiaan Huygens","Galileo Galilei","Johannes Kepler"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens (1629-1695) first published a thorough analysis of centripetal acceleration in 1673.\nNewton proposed the term 'centripetal', meaning 'centre-seeking', and probably knew the result some years earlier. He did not publish the first thorough analysis, so option (1) is tempting but wrong.\nGalileo is linked with the independence of horizontal and vertical motion in projectiles, not with circular motion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an obliquely launched projectile, the speed is minimum, but not zero, at the highest point of its path.\nReason (R): The horizontal component of velocity of a projectile stays constant throughout its flight, while the vertical component becomes zero at the highest point.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: since $a_x = 0$, $v_x = v_0\\cos\\theta_0$ is constant. The vertical component $v_y = v_0\\sin\\theta_0 - gt$ becomes zero at $t_m$.\nA is true: speed $= \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$ is smallest when $v_y = 0$. At that point the speed equals $v_0\\cos\\theta_0 \\neq 0$.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about resolving vectors into rectangular components is correct?","image":"","options":["The component $A_x$ of a vector $\\vec{A}$ is itself a vector directed along the x-axis.","A unit vector carries the same dimension and unit as the vector it points along.","For a position vector $\\vec{r} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j} + z\\hat{k}$, the coordinates x, y, z are its components and $|\\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$.","Every rectangular component of a vector is positive, because the magnitude of a vector is always positive."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $A_x$ is a real number. Only $A_x\\hat{i}$ is a vector.\n(2) Incorrect: a unit vector has no dimension and no unit. It only specifies a direction.\n(3) Correct: the coordinates of the point are the components of $\\vec{r}$. Its magnitude follows from $A = \\sqrt{A_x^2+A_y^2+A_z^2}$.\n(4) Incorrect: for example, $A_x = A\\cos\\theta$ is negative when $90^\\circ < \\theta < 270^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle is $\\vec{r} = (4t\\,\\hat{i} + 3t^2\\,\\hat{j})$ m, where t is in seconds. What is the speed of the particle at t = 2 s?","image":"","options":["12.6 m/s","16.0 m/s","10.0 m/s","14.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 4t$ and $y = 3t^2$.\nFormula: $v_x = \\frac{dx}{dt}$, $v_y = \\frac{dy}{dt}$, and $v = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$.\nSubstituting: $v_x = 4$ m/s and $v_y = 6t = 12$ m/s at t = 2 s, so $v = \\sqrt{16 + 144} = \\sqrt{160}$.\nSo the speed is about 12.6 m/s. Adding the components (16 m/s) is wrong because they are perpendicular.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Volume\n(b) Impulse\n(c) Angular frequency\n(d) Angular velocity\nList-II:\n(P) Vector; product of a force and the time for which it acts\n(Q) Scalar; obtained by multiplying lengths\n(R) Vector; direction related to the sense of rotation\n(S) Scalar; equal to $2\\pi\\nu$","image":"","options":["(a)-Q, (b)-P, (c)-S, (d)-R","(a)-Q, (b)-S, (c)-P, (d)-R","(a)-S, (b)-P, (c)-Q, (d)-R","(a)-Q, (b)-P, (c)-R, (d)-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Volume: a product of lengths with no direction, so it is a scalar → Q.\n(b) Impulse: force × time, where a vector is multiplied by a scalar, so it is a vector → P.\n(c) Angular frequency: $\\omega = 2\\pi\\nu$, a pure magnitude, so it is a scalar → S.\n(d) Angular velocity: has a direction linked to the sense of rotation, so it is a vector → R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For two non-zero vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$, the magnitude $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}|$ can be equal to $|\\vec{A}| + |\\vec{B}|$.\nReason (R): $\\vec{A} - \\vec{B} = \\vec{A} + (-\\vec{B})$. When $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ point in opposite directions, $\\vec{A}$ and $-\\vec{B}$ point in the same direction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Subtraction is defined as adding the negative vector. Reversing $\\vec{B}$ makes $-\\vec{B}$ parallel to $\\vec{A}$ when $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ are antiparallel.\nA is true. Two parallel vectors add to give a magnitude equal to the sum of their magnitudes. So $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}| = |\\vec{A}| + |\\vec{B}|$ for antiparallel $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$.\nR directly shows when the equality in $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}| \\le |\\vec{A}| + |\\vec{B}|$ holds, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the equality of two vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\vec{A} = \\vec{B}$ if they have the same magnitude, whatever their directions","$\\vec{A} = \\vec{B}$ only if their tails are located at the same point","$\\vec{A} = \\vec{B}$ if, and only if, they have the same magnitude and the same direction","$\\vec{A} = \\vec{B}$ if they lie along the same line, even if they point in opposite directions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT: two vectors are equal if, and only if, they have the same magnitude and the same direction.\n(1) is wrong. Vectors of equal length but different directions, like A′ and B′ in Fig. 3.2(b), are unequal.\n(2) is wrong. A free vector may be shifted parallel to itself without changing it, so the tails need not coincide.\n(4) is wrong. Opposite directions make the vectors unequal.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a moving object, the magnitude of displacement between two points can never exceed the path length between those points.\nReason (R): Displacement depends only on the initial and final positions, while path length depends on the actual path followed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Displacement is the straight line joining the initial and final positions, and a straight line is the shortest route between two points. So the path length is always greater than or equal to the magnitude of displacement.\nR is true. It is exactly this distinction (end points only versus the actual path) that leads to A, so R correctly explains A.\nThe two are equal only when the object does not change direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A point on the rim of a wheel of radius 0.5 m moves in a circle with a frequency of 2 revolutions per second. What is its linear speed? (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)","image":"","options":["6.28 m/s","3.14 m/s","12.56 m/s","1.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.5 m, $\\nu$ = 2 s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $v = 2\\pi R\\nu$\nSubstituting: $v = 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 0.5 \\times 2$\nSo v = 6.28 m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A force of magnitude 20 N acts in the x-y plane. It makes an angle of $150^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise. What is the x-component of this force?","image":"","options":["17.3 N","10 N","$-17.3$ N","$-10$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: F = 20 N, θ = $150^\\circ$ with the +x axis.\nFormula: $F_x = F\\cos\\theta$\nSubstituting: $F_x = 20\\cos150^\\circ = 20 \\times (-0.866)$\nSo $F_x \\approx -17.3$ N. The component is negative because the force points into the second quadrant. The value −10 N would come from wrongly using $\\sin$ with a negative sign.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A stone tied to a string of length 0.5 m is whirled in a horizontal circle at constant speed. It completes 30 revolutions in 15 s. What is the magnitude of its centripetal acceleration? (Take $\\pi^2 = 9.87$)","image":"","options":["79 m/s$^2$","39.5 m/s$^2$","158 m/s$^2$","12.6 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.5 m, $\\nu$ = 30/15 = 2 s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $a_c = 4\\pi^2\\nu^2 R$\nSubstituting: $a_c = 4 \\times 9.87 \\times 2^2 \\times 0.5 = 78.96$\nSo $a_c \\approx$ 79 m/s$^2$, directed towards the centre.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two balls are projected from the same point on level ground with the same speed, one at $30^\\circ$ and the other at $60^\\circ$ to the horizontal. They land at the same point, and the ratio of their maximum heights is 1 : 3.\nReason (R): The horizontal range is proportional to $\\sin 2\\theta_0$, which has the same value for $\\theta_0$ and $(90^\\circ - \\theta_0)$, while the maximum height is proportional to $\\sin^2\\theta_0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $R = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin2\\theta_0}{g}$ and $h_m = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta_0}{2g}$.\nRanges: $\\sin60^\\circ = \\sin120^\\circ$, so the ranges are equal and the balls land at the same point.\nHeights: $\\sin^2 30^\\circ : \\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} : \\frac{3}{4} = 1:3$. So A is true.\nBoth parts of A follow directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following physical quantities are vectors?\nimpulse, work, angular velocity, density, volume, pressure","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Impulse is a vector. It is force multiplied by time and has the direction of the force.\nWork is a scalar.\nAngular velocity is a vector.\nDensity is a scalar (mass/volume).\nVolume is a scalar (a product of lengths).\nPressure is a scalar. It acts equally in all directions and has no single direction.\nVectors: impulse and angular velocity, so the count is 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle moving in a plane can have a non-zero acceleration even though its speed remains constant throughout the motion.\nReason (R): Acceleration is the rate of change of the velocity vector, and the velocity changes whenever its direction changes, even if its magnitude does not.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Uniform circular motion is an example: the speed is constant, but there is a centripetal acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{v^2}{R}$.\nR is true. Since $\\vec{a} = \\frac{d\\vec{v}}{dt}$, a change in the direction of $\\vec{v}$ alone gives a non-zero $\\Delta \\vec{v}$.\nR explains A directly, because the acceleration exists only due to the change in direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following physical quantities are vectors?\nmass, volume, impulse, temperature, angular velocity, density, displacement, electric current","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A vector has magnitude and direction and obeys the triangle (parallelogram) law of addition.\nScalars: mass, volume, temperature and density each need only a number with a unit.\nElectric current is also a scalar. It has a sense of flow, but currents meeting at a junction add algebraically, not by the triangle law.\nVectors: impulse (along the force), angular velocity (along the axis of rotation) and displacement.\nSo there are 3 vectors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about vector addition and subtraction are correct?\n(i) $\\vec{A} + \\vec{B} = \\vec{B} + \\vec{A}$\n(ii) $\\vec{A} - \\vec{B} = \\vec{B} - \\vec{A}$\n(iii) $(\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}) + \\vec{C} = \\vec{A} + (\\vec{B} + \\vec{C})$\n(iv) $\\vec{A} + (-\\vec{A})$ is a vector of zero magnitude directed along $\\vec{A}$\n(v) In the parallelogram method, the resultant lies along the diagonal drawn from the common tail of the two vectors","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: vector addition is commutative.\n(ii) Incorrect: $\\vec{A} - \\vec{B} = -(\\vec{B} - \\vec{A})$, so the two vectors are opposite in direction.\n(iii) Correct: vector addition obeys the associative law.\n(iv) Incorrect: the result is the null vector, and its direction cannot be specified.\n(v) Correct: the resultant runs from the common origin O along the diagonal OS.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Multiplying a constant velocity vector by a time duration gives a displacement vector.\nReason (R): When a vector $\\vec{A}$ is multiplied by a scalar $\\lambda$ having its own physical dimension, the dimension of $\\lambda\\vec{A}$ is the product of the dimensions of $\\lambda$ and $\\vec{A}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT states that the dimension of $\\lambda\\vec{A}$ is the product of the dimensions of $\\lambda$ and $\\vec{A}$.\nA is true. Time is a positive scalar, so the direction of the velocity is kept. The dimension becomes $[T][LT^{-1}] = [L]$, which is the dimension of displacement.\nR therefore directly explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A unit vector has no dimension and no unit.\n(ii) The x-component $A_x$ of a vector $\\vec{A}$ is itself a vector.\n(iii) Multiplying any vector by the number zero gives a null vector.\n(iv) Two vectors having equal magnitudes are always equal vectors.\n(v) Vector addition obeys both the commutative and the associative laws.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a unit vector only specifies a direction.\n(ii) Incorrect: $A_x$ is a real number. Only $A_x\\hat{i}$ is a vector.\n(iii) Correct: $0\\vec{A} = \\vec{0}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: equal vectors need both the same magnitude and the same direction.\n(v) Correct: $\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}=\\vec{B}+\\vec{A}$ and $(\\vec{A}+\\vec{B})+\\vec{C}=\\vec{A}+(\\vec{B}+\\vec{C})$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two stones thrown from the same point with equal speeds at $25^\\circ$ and $65^\\circ$ to the horizontal land at the same point on level ground.\nReason (R): For angles $45^\\circ + \\alpha$ and $45^\\circ - \\alpha$, the values of $\\sin 2\\theta_0$ are equal, both being $\\cos 2\\alpha$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The angles $25^\\circ$ and $65^\\circ$ are $45^\\circ - 20^\\circ$ and $45^\\circ + 20^\\circ$.\nR is true: $\\sin(90^\\circ \\pm 2\\alpha) = \\cos 2\\alpha$.\nSince $R = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$, equal speeds and equal $\\sin 2\\theta_0$ give equal ranges. So A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is true for the unit vectors $\\hat{i}$, $\\hat{j}$ and $\\hat{k}$?","image":"","options":["They have magnitude 1 m and the unit of length","They have unit magnitude, no dimension and no unit, and only specify a direction","Their magnitudes depend on the vector they are associated with","They are mutually parallel vectors along the x-axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a unit vector as a vector of unit magnitude that points in a particular direction.\nA unit vector has no dimension and no unit. It only specifies a direction.\n$\\hat{i}$, $\\hat{j}$ and $\\hat{k}$ lie along the x-, y- and z-axes and are mutually perpendicular.\nOption (1) is tempting, but it wrongly gives the unit vectors a unit of length.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a particle moving between two points, the magnitude of its displacement can never exceed the path length covered.\nReason (R): Displacement depends only on the initial and final positions and is the straight line joining them, whatever the actual path.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: the displacement vector joins the initial and final positions directly. It does not depend on the path taken.\nA is true: a straight line is the shortest distance between two points. Any actual path is therefore at least as long as the displacement.\nThe two are equal only when the object does not change direction.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the resolution of a vector $\\vec{A} = A_x\\hat{i} + A_y\\hat{j}$ is incorrect?","image":"","options":["A unit vector has no dimension and no unit; it only specifies a direction","$A_x$ is itself a vector directed along the x-axis","A component such as $A_x = A\\cos\\theta$ can be positive, negative or zero depending on $\\theta$","The vector $\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}$ has magnitude $\\sqrt{2}$ and makes $45^\\circ$ with the x-axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. A unit vector is dimensionless and only gives a direction.\n(2) Incorrect. $A_x$ is a real number, a component. The vector along the x-axis is $A_x\\hat{i}$.\n(3) Correct. $\\cos\\theta$ can be positive, negative or zero.\n(4) Correct. The magnitude is $\\sqrt{1^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{2}$, and $\\tan\\theta = 1$ gives $45^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Rain is falling vertically downward at 24 m/s. A wind then starts blowing horizontally at 7 m/s. What is the magnitude of the resultant velocity of the raindrops?","image":"","options":["25 m/s","31 m/s","17 m/s","22.5 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_r = 24$ m/s (vertical) and $v_w = 7$ m/s (horizontal). The two are perpendicular.\nFormula: $R = \\sqrt{v_r^2 + v_w^2}$\nSubstituting: $R = \\sqrt{576 + 49} = \\sqrt{625}$\nSo R = 25 m/s. Note that 31 m/s would be correct only if the two velocities were parallel.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves with constant speed along a circle of radius 3 m. In 2 s it travels along an arc that subtends an angle of $120^\\circ$ at the centre. What is the ratio of its average speed to the magnitude of its average velocity over these 2 s?","image":"","options":["1.21","1.00","1.57","0.83"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 3 m, arc angle = $120^\\circ = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ rad, t = 2 s.\nFormula: arc length = $R\\theta$ and chord = $2R\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}$.\nSubstituting: arc = $3 \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi$ ≈ 6.28 m, and chord = $2 \\times 3 \\times \\sin 60^\\circ = 3\\sqrt{3}$ ≈ 5.20 m.\nThe time is the same for both quantities, so the ratio = 6.28/5.20 ≈ 1.21.\nThe ratio must be at least 1, which rules out 0.83.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A projectile is launched from level ground with speed $v_0$ at angle $\\theta_0$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Time to reach maximum height\n(b) Time of flight\n(c) Maximum height\n(d) Horizontal range\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$\n(ii) $\\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$\n(iii) $\\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$\n(iv) $\\frac{2v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Set $v_y = v_0\\sin\\theta_0 - gt_m = 0$, which gives $t_m = \\frac{v_0\\sin\\theta_0}{g}$ → (ii).\n(b) By symmetry of the path, $T_f = 2t_m$ → (iv).\n(c) Substituting $t_m$ into the y-equation gives $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$ → (iii).\n(d) $R = (v_0\\cos\\theta_0)T_f = \\frac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\theta_0}{g}$ → (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two displacement vectors are $\\vec{A} = (3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m and $\\vec{B} = (5\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j})$ m. What is the magnitude of their resultant $\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}$?","image":"","options":["8.25 m","10.0 m","7.21 m","9.43 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_x = 3, A_y = 4, B_x = 5, B_y = -2$ (in m).\nFormula: $R_x = A_x + B_x$, $R_y = A_y + B_y$, and $R = \\sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2}$.\nSubstituting: $R_x = 8$ m, $R_y = 2$ m, so $R = \\sqrt{64 + 4} = \\sqrt{68}$.\nSo $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}| \\approx 8.25$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves in a circle of radius 5 m with a constant speed of 10 m/s. What is the magnitude of its average acceleration while it travels half a revolution (from one end of a diameter to the other)?","image":"","options":["20.0 m/s^2","0 m/s^2","12.7 m/s^2","6.4 m/s^2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: R = 5 m, v = 10 m/s\nTime for half a revolution: $\\frac{\\pi R}{v} = \\frac{5\\pi}{10} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ s\nThe velocity reverses direction, so $|\\Delta\\vec{v}| = 2v = 20$ m/s\nFormula: average acceleration $= \\frac{|\\Delta\\vec{v}|}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{20}{\\pi/2} = \\frac{40}{\\pi} \\approx 12.7$ m/s^2\nThe value 20 m/s^2 is the instantaneous centripetal acceleration $\\frac{v^2}{R}$, not the average. The value 0 applies only over one full cycle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Multiplying any vector $\\vec{A}$ by zero gives the null vector, whose direction cannot be specified.\n(ii) The unit vector along a velocity vector has the unit m s$^{-1}$.\n(iii) Multiplying a constant velocity vector by a time interval gives a displacement vector.\n(iv) $\\frac{\\vec{A}}{|\\vec{A}|}$ has magnitude 1 and points in the same direction as $\\vec{A}$.\n(v) For any vector $\\vec{A}$, $\\vec{A} + \\vec{0} = \\vec{0}$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $0\\vec{A} = \\vec{0}$. Its magnitude is zero, so its direction cannot be specified.\n(ii) Incorrect: a unit vector has no dimension and no unit. It only specifies a direction.\n(iii) Correct: the dimension of $\\lambda\\vec{A}$ is the product of the dimensions of $\\lambda$ and $\\vec{A}$. So velocity × time gives a displacement.\n(iv) Correct: since $\\vec{A} = |\\vec{A}|\\hat{n}$, we get $\\hat{n} = \\frac{\\vec{A}}{|\\vec{A}|}$, a unit vector along $\\vec{A}$.\n(v) Incorrect: the correct property is $\\vec{A} + \\vec{0} = \\vec{A}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Multiplying a vector $\\vec{A}$ by $-1.5$ gives a vector opposite in direction to $\\vec{A}$ whose magnitude is $1.5|\\vec{A}|$.\nStatement II: The null vector obtained from $\\vec{A} + (-\\vec{A})$ points along the direction of $\\vec{A}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Multiplying by a negative number $-\\lambda$ reverses the direction and scales the magnitude by $\\lambda$.\nStatement II is incorrect. The null vector has zero magnitude, so no direction can be specified for it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For motion along a straight line, the angle between the velocity and the acceleration of an object is either 0° or 180°.\nStatement II: For motion in a plane, the angle between the velocity and acceleration vectors can have any value from 0° to 180°.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In one dimension, v and a lie along the same line. They point either the same way (0°) or opposite ways (180°).\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that in two or three dimensions, v and a may have any angle between 0° and 180°. A projectile is an example: the angle keeps changing during flight.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A vector is given by $\\vec{A} = 2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j} + 6\\hat{k}$. If $\\gamma$ is the angle that $\\vec{A}$ makes with the z-axis, what is the value of $\\cos\\gamma$?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{7}$","$\\frac{3}{7}$","$\\frac{6}{7}$","$\\frac{6}{11}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $A_x = 2$, $A_y = 3$, $A_z = 6$.\nMagnitude: $A = \\sqrt{A_x^2 + A_y^2 + A_z^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 9 + 36} = 7$\nFormula: $A_z = A\\cos\\gamma$, so $\\cos\\gamma = A_z/A$.\nSubstituting: $\\cos\\gamma = 6/7 \\approx 0.857$. The value $6/11$ comes from wrongly dividing by the sum of the components.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moving in the x-y plane is at position $\\vec{r}_1 = (2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j})$ m at one instant. A little later it is at $\\vec{r}_2 = (8\\hat{i} - 5\\hat{j})$ m. What is the magnitude of its displacement?","image":"","options":["10 m","14 m","5.8 m","13.0 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{r}_1 = 2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}$, $\\vec{r}_2 = 8\\hat{i} - 5\\hat{j}$ (in m).\nFormula: $\\Delta\\vec{r} = \\vec{r}_2 - \\vec{r}_1 = \\Delta x\\,\\hat{i} + \\Delta y\\,\\hat{j}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta x = 6$ m and $\\Delta y = -8$ m, so $|\\Delta\\vec{r}| = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2}$.\nSo the displacement is 10 m. Subtracting the magnitudes $|\\vec{r}_2| - |\\vec{r}_1|$ gives 5.8 m, which is wrong because vectors must be subtracted component-wise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Two non-zero vectors $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ have equal magnitudes. How many of the following statements are always correct?\n(i) $(\\vec{A} + \\vec{B})$ is perpendicular to $(\\vec{A} - \\vec{B})$ (whenever both are non-zero).\n(ii) If $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}| = |\\vec{A}|$, the angle between $\\vec{A}$ and $\\vec{B}$ is $120^\\circ$.\n(iii) $|\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}| \\le 2|\\vec{A}|$.\n(iv) $|\\vec{A} + \\vec{B}| = |\\vec{A} - \\vec{B}|$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $(\\vec{A}+\\vec{B})\\cdot(\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}) = A^2 - B^2 = 0$. Equivalently, the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular.\n(ii) Correct: $A^2 = A^2 + A^2 + 2A^2\\cos\\theta$ gives $\\cos\\theta = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so $\\theta = 120^\\circ$.\n(iii) Correct: $|\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}| \\le A + B = 2A$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $|\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}|^2 - |\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}|^2 = 4AB\\cos\\theta$, which is zero only when $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (vector relation) with List-II (what it expresses).\nList-I:\n(P) $\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}=\\vec{B}+\\vec{A}$\n(Q) $(\\vec{A}+\\vec{B})+\\vec{C}=\\vec{A}+(\\vec{B}+\\vec{C})$\n(R) $\\vec{A}+(-\\vec{A})=\\vec{0}$\n(S) $\\vec{A}-\\vec{B}=\\vec{A}+(-\\vec{B})$\nList-II:\n(1) Associative law of addition\n(2) Null vector\n(3) Definition of vector subtraction\n(4) Commutative law of addition","image":"","options":["P-1, Q-4, R-2, S-3","P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2","P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"P: Changing the order of the two vectors does not change their sum. This is the commutative law, so P-4.\nQ: Changing the grouping does not change the sum. This is the associative law, so Q-1.\nR: Two equal and opposite vectors add to a vector of zero magnitude, the null vector. So R-2.\nS: NCERT defines subtraction as adding the negative of $\\vec{B}$. So S-3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"The vector $\\vec{A} = 7\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j}$ is to be resolved along the two non-collinear vectors $\\vec{a} = 2\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}$ and $\\vec{b} = \\hat{i} - \\hat{j}$, so that $\\vec{A} = \\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b}$. What are the values of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$?","image":"","options":["$\\lambda = 1$, $\\mu = 3$","$\\lambda = 3$, $\\mu = 1$","$\\lambda = 2$, $\\mu = 3$","$\\lambda = 3$, $\\mu = -1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $\\lambda\\vec{a} + \\mu\\vec{b} = (2\\lambda + \\mu)\\hat{i} + (\\lambda - \\mu)\\hat{j}$.\nEquate this to $7\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j}$: $2\\lambda + \\mu = 7$ and $\\lambda - \\mu = 2$.\nAdding the two equations gives $3\\lambda = 9$, so $\\lambda = 3$ and then $\\mu = 1$.\nCheck: $3(2\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}) + (\\hat{i} - \\hat{j}) = 7\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A stone is thrown with a speed of 30 m/s at $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Taking $g = 10$ m/s$^2$, find the maximum height it reaches above the point of projection.","image":"","options":["11.25 m","22.5 m","33.75 m","45 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_0 = 30$ m/s, $\\theta_0 = 30^\\circ$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $h_m = \\frac{(v_0\\sin\\theta_0)^2}{2g}$\nSubstituting: $h_m = \\frac{(30 \\times 0.5)^2}{2 \\times 10} = \\frac{225}{20}$\nSo $h_m$ = 11.25 m. (22.5 m comes from forgetting the factor 2; 33.75 m comes from using $\\cos\\theta_0$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A small bead moves steadily on a circular wire loop of radius 0.5 m and completes 2 revolutions every second. What is the magnitude of its centripetal acceleration? (Take $\\pi^2 \\approx 9.87$)","image":"","options":["78.96 m/s$^2$","39.48 m/s$^2$","12.57 m/s$^2$","157.9 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.5 m, frequency $\\nu$ = 2 rev/s.\nFormula: $a_c = \\omega^2 R = 4\\pi^2\\nu^2 R$.\nSubstituting: $a_c = 4 \\times 9.87 \\times 2^2 \\times 0.5$.\nThis gives $a_c = 4 \\times 9.87 \\times 4 \\times 0.5 \\approx 78.96$ m/s$^2$.\nThe option 39.48 m/s$^2$ comes from using $\\nu$ in place of $\\nu^2$. The option 157.9 m/s$^2$ comes from leaving out R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-PLANE-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Motion in a Plane","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Motion in a Plane","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves in a circle of radius 2 m with a constant speed of 4 m/s. What is the magnitude of its average acceleration while it covers one quarter of the circle?","image":"","options":["7.20 m/s$^2$","8.00 m/s$^2$","0 m/s$^2$","5.66 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 2 m, v = 4 m/s.\nOver a quarter turn, the initial and final velocities are perpendicular. So $|\\Delta\\mathbf{v}| = \\sqrt{v^2 + v^2} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.66$ m/s.\nTime taken: $\\Delta t = \\frac{\\pi R/2}{v} = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\approx 0.785$ s.\nSo $|\\mathbf{a}_{avg}| = \\frac{5.66}{0.785} \\approx$ 7.20 m/s$^2$.\nThis is less than the instantaneous value $v^2/R$ = 8 m/s$^2$. Option (4), 5.66, is $|\\Delta\\mathbf{v}|$ in m/s, not an acceleration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 3 · Motion in a Plane","difficulty":"hard"}]});
